title: history_context
---
// The History dimension's people (`docs/planning/game/history-dimension.md`):
// Clio, the Muse of History, who keeps the gate in the Cradle, and the people
// of each room of the grid. One node per resident, named after its spec
// (`history_<resident>`).
//
// The dimension is a grid: east is later, and each floor is a region of the
// world, so the people of one column lived at the same time. Each person
// knows their own time and place well and is often wrong about the others.
// The jokes are on the famous and on how history is told, never on a people.
// The people of prehistory are original, and they are told with respect.
//
// Three choices here set flags: Socratease's answer opens the stoa gate
// (`history_knows_nothing`); Genghis Can opens the gate in the Great Wall
// (`history_can_says_yes`); Genghis Can't puts up the scaffold over it
// (`history_cant_says_maybe`).
===

title: history_clio
---
Clio: Oh good, a visitor. Welcome to the beginning. Well, a beginning. Historians argue about where to start. Historians argue about everything.
-> Who are you?
    Clio: Clio. The Muse of History. I keep the record. It is very long and nobody reads the footnotes.
-> Where am I?
    Clio: The Great Rift Valley, in Africa, about three hundred thousand years ago. Everyone you will meet in this dimension has ancestors in Africa. Not in one valley: the oldest bones of our kind are from Morocco, Ethiopia and South Africa. Africa is the cradle, all of it.
    -> Everyone?
        Clio: Everyone. People walked out of Africa and kept walking, to every land there is. That is the first story, and it is the same story for all of them.
    -> Go on.
        Clio: Fire, which people had tended for hundreds of thousands of years already. Then the long walk. Then, in many places at once, farming. Then cities, and then the trouble starts. Writing comes in around the trouble. Then me.
-> How does this place work?
    Clio: East is later. Every floor is a different part of the world, and the rooms one above the other happened at the same time. A way up or down joins them where people met, and where the atlas has folded the map to make a road, a sign says so. History is not a line. It is a lot of lines that keep running into each other.
-> Goodbye.
    Clio: Keep the fire lit before the hyenas come. And remember: the past is not over. It is just upstream.
===

title: history_hammurabbi
---
Hammurabbi: Halt. Have you read the code?
-> What code?
    Hammurabbi: The Code. Two hundred and eighty-two laws, carved in stone so nobody can say they did not see them. An eye for an eye. A tooth for a tooth. A receipt for a receipt.
-> What happens if I break a law?
    Hammurabbi: It depends who you are. A noble pays a fine. A commoner pays more. It is very fair, if you are a noble.
-> How do I cross the canal?
    Hammurabbi: Open the sluice. The reed bridge rises with the water. Law forty-eight says a farmer who lets his dyke break pays for the flood. So be careful with the sluice.
-> Goodbye.
    Hammurabbi: Go in justice. Pay at the granary.
===

title: history_gilgamash
---
Gilgamash: I am Gilgamash, king of Uruk, two thirds god, one third man, and all of me is sad.
-> Why are you sad?
    Gilgamash: My friend Enkidu died. So I went to the end of the world to find the plant of eternal life. I found it. Then I took a nap. A snake ate it.
-> Is that the first story ever?
    Gilgamash: One of the first ever written down. And look what it is about. A man who cannot stop thinking about dying. Some things do not change.
-> What should I do with my life?
    Gilgamash: Eat. Wash. Wear clean clothes. Hold the hand of a friend. The tavern keeper told me that, at the end of the world. She was right. I still went looking for the plant.
-> Goodbye.
    Gilgamash: Build a wall. People remember walls.
===

title: history_scribe_of_debts
---
Scribe of Debts: Name? Village? How many sheep?
-> I do not have any sheep.
    Scribe of Debts: Then you owe sheep. That is how it works. I write it on the tablet, and then it is true.
-> Why did people start writing?
    Scribe of Debts: Poetry, people think. No. Barley. Who gave how much barley to the temple, and who owes how much back. The first writing is a receipt. The second is a reminder that the first is overdue.
-> What is money?
    Scribe of Debts: A promise that everyone agrees to believe. Before coins there was credit. Before credit there was a scribe with a very good memory and a very sharp stylus.
-> Goodbye.
    Scribe of Debts: Interest is due at the harvest. It is always due at the harvest.
===

title: history_hatshepsut_hats
---
Hat-shepsut: Behold. The pharaoh. Yes, this is the pharaoh's beard. It is on a string. All pharaohs' beards are on a string. Mine just has more string.
-> Why so many hats?
    Hat-shepsut: The white crown of Upper Egypt, the red crown of Lower Egypt, the double crown of both, the blue war crown, and a sun hat. A pharaoh must be ready for anything.
-> Were you a good pharaoh?
    Hat-shepsut: Twenty years of peace. Trade expeditions to the land of Punt. The best temples in the valley. Then the next pharaoh chiselled my name off the walls. History is written by whoever has the chisel.
-> How do I cross the Nile?
    Hat-shepsut: Take the felucca. It goes back and forth all day. The crocodiles go back and forth too, below it. Do not fall in.
-> Goodbye.
    Hat-shepsut: Remember my name. Somebody has to.
===

title: history_ramesses_pretty_good
---
Ramesses the Pretty Good: Have you seen my statue? This is a small one. I have bigger ones. I have the biggest ones.
-> Why do you carry a statue of yourself?
    Ramesses the Pretty Good: In case I meet someone who has not seen one. It happens less often than you would think. I put them everywhere.
-> Did you win the battle of Kadesh?
    Ramesses the Pretty Good: I wrote on every temple wall that I did. The Hittites wrote that they did. Really it was a draw, and then we signed the first peace treaty in history. That part I did not carve as big.
-> What is in the pyramid?
    Ramesses the Pretty Good: An old king's tomb. And mummies, who are not happy to be woken. Everything you need for the afterlife is in there. Do not take it. Well. Do not get caught.
-> Goodbye.
    Ramesses the Pretty Good: Tell them you met Ramesses. The Great. Pretty great. Okay.
===

title: history_socratease
---
Socratease: Ah, a traveller. Tell me: do you wish to pass through the stoa?
-> Yes.
    Socratease: But what is it to pass? And who is the one who passes? Before the gate opens, answer me one question. What do you know?
    -> Everything.
        Socratease: Everything? Then tell me what virtue is. No? Then perhaps you do not know everything. Think again.
    -> How to jump very well.
        Socratease: A fine skill. But is jumping knowledge, or only a habit of the legs? Think again.
    -> I know that I know nothing.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "history_knows_nothing" true>>
        Socratease: At last. That is the beginning of wisdom. The gate is open. Go and ask somebody else something annoying.
    -> What do you know?
        Socratease: Ha. A question for a question. You learn fast. But I asked first. Think again.
-> Why do you only ask questions?
    Socratease: Do I? And is a question not also a kind of answer?
-> What happened to you?
    Socratease: The city put me on trial for asking too many questions of the young. They gave me hemlock. I asked what was in it. They did not like that question either.
-> Goodbye.
    Socratease: Is it goodbye? Or only the end of this conversation?
===

title: history_herodotoss
---
Herodotoss: Greetings! Herodotoss of Halicarnassus. Father of History. Some say also the father of lies. Those people are not invited to my readings.
-> What is history?
    Herodotoss: The word means inquiry. I went and asked people what happened. Then I wrote down what they said. Then I wrote down what I thought of what they said. Then I added the bit about the giant gold-digging ants.
-> Giant ants?
    Herodotoss: In India, ants the size of foxes dig up gold. A Persian told me. Well, he said it about marmots. Ants, marmots. The gold is the important part.
-> Who won, the Persians or the Greeks?
    Herodotoss: The Greeks, at Marathon and Salamis. But the Persians had the largest empire the world had ever seen, with roads and post and tolerance for every god. I try to be fair. It annoys everyone.
-> Goodbye.
    Herodotoss: Tell people what you saw. Then tell them a little more.
===

title: history_julius_sneezer
---
Julius Sneezer: Ah... ah... AH-VE! Hail. I came, I saw, I sneezed.
-> Bless you.
    Julius Sneezer: Thank you. Nobody else in the senate says it. They just stand behind me. With knives. Et tu, tissue?
-> Did Rome fall?
    Julius Sneezer: In the west, slowly, over about two hundred years. In the east it lasted another thousand. Rome did not fall. It wore out unevenly, like a toga.
-> What is in the colosseum?
    Julius Sneezer: The games. Soldiers of every age that ever fought for Rome or against it. Win, and you get a seat in the emperor's box. And a thumb. Up, hopefully.
-> Goodbye.
    Julius Sneezer: Beware the Ides of March. And the pollen of April.
===

title: history_marco_polo_neck
---
Marco Polo-neck: Marco! You are supposed to say Polo. Nobody ever says Polo.
-> Polo.
    Marco Polo-neck: Thank you. Seventeen years at the court of the Great Khan and that is the first time.
-> Where have you been?
    Marco Polo-neck: Everywhere on this road. Paper money! Coal that burns like wood! Cities with twelve thousand bridges! I wrote it all in a book. Nobody in Venice believes a word.
-> What is the Silk Road?
    Marco Polo-neck: Not one road. Many roads, and many middlemen. Silk went west, silver went east, and ideas, religions and diseases went every way at once. Mostly nobody walked the whole thing. Except me. Allegedly.
-> Goodbye.
    Marco Polo-neck: Marco! ... Nothing? Fine.
===

title: history_confusius
---
Confusius: Ah. Young traveller. I am the sage Confusius. I am... honestly not sure about very much.
-> Teach me something.
    Confusius: Respect your elders. Your elders should deserve it. Rulers should be good so that the people are good. That is the whole thing. It is harder than it sounds. Most rulers do not get past the first part.
-> Why are you confused?
    Confusius: I wrote about order, harmony and proper relationships. Then the next two thousand years of emperors quoted me to explain whatever they were already doing. I find that confusing.
-> How do I cross the quicksand?
    Confusius: A wise traveller rides the camel. A foolish traveller walks. A very foolish traveller asks the camel for directions.
-> Goodbye.
    Confusius: The journey of a thousand miles begins with... somebody else said that. I forget who.
===

title: history_genghis_can
---
Genghis Can: The Wall. Can I get through it? I can. Can you? With me, you can.
-> Open the gate.
    <<command "world.set_flag" "history_can_says_yes" true>>
    Genghis Can: Yes. Done. The gate is open. The garrison is behind it, and they are not happy about it. That is the short road. Short roads are where the fighting is.
-> Is there another way?
    Genghis Can: Probably. My brother will find it. My brother finds every other way. It takes him a while.
-> How did you build an empire?
    Genghis Can: Horses. Discipline. Promoting people for being good and not for being cousins. And saying yes before anyone else had finished saying maybe.
-> Goodbye.
    Genghis Can: Ride well. Then ride further.
===

title: history_genghis_cant
---
Genghis Can't: Through the Wall? We could. I mean, my brother could. I have concerns. There is a garrison, and they have arrows.
-> Is there another way?
    Genghis Can't: Always. I had the men put up a scaffold on the west face. Up, over the top, along the rampart, and down the far side, over their heads. It is longer.
    -> Put up the scaffold.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "history_cant_says_maybe" true>>
        Genghis Can't: There. Up and over. We arrive at the same place, later. Later is underrated.
    -> I will think about it.
        Genghis Can't: Good. Thinking about it is my whole method.
-> Why are you so careful?
    Genghis Can't: Somebody has to check the map. The Pax Mongolica, the post relay, the trade roads that were safe from one end of Asia to the other: that was planning. Conquering is the easy part. Running it is the hard part.
-> Goodbye.
    Genghis Can't: Take water. Take a spare horse. Take a spare spare horse.
===

title: history_kublai_khant_wait
---
Kublai Khan't-Wait: You are late. Everyone is late. I am emperor of China and khan of khans and I have been waiting since breakfast.
-> What are you waiting for?
    Kublai Khan't-Wait: The fleet to Japan. Twice it sailed. Twice a storm sank it. The Japanese call the storm the divine wind. I call it the second time I have had to wait for a fleet.
-> What are those notes in your hands?
    Kublai Khan't-Wait: Paper money. Much faster than carrying silver. I printed lots of it. Then it was worth less. Nobody warned me. Well, everyone warned me. I did not wait for them to finish.
-> What is the Yam?
    Kublai Khan't-Wait: The post. A station every day's ride, with fresh horses. A message crosses the whole empire in weeks. In my opinion, still too slow.
-> Goodbye.
    Kublai Khan't-Wait: Go. Quickly. Why are you still here?
===

title: history_mansa_moolah
---
Mansa Moolah: Welcome to Mali! Have some gold. No, take it. Take more. I insist.
-> Thank you?
    Mansa Moolah: On my pilgrimage to Mecca I gave away so much gold in Cairo that the price of gold there fell for twelve years. I was trying to be generous. I was also, accidentally, a lesson in economics.
-> Are you the richest person ever?
    Mansa Moolah: People say so. It is hard to measure. Half the gold of the known world came through Mali. Also the salt. Salt was worth its weight in gold here, which made the gold confused.
-> What is in the library?
    Mansa Moolah: Books. I brought scholars and architects home with me and built schools in Timbuktu. Gold runs out. Books do not. Well, they do, but slower.
-> Goodbye.
    Mansa Moolah: Wait! You forgot your gold. Here. Never mind, it is worth less now.
===

title: history_ibn_batootah
---
Ibn Batootah: Peace be upon you, traveller. I am Ibn Batootah. I left home for a pilgrimage. That was twenty-nine years ago.
-> Where did you go?
    Ibn Batootah: Mecca, then Persia, then the steppe, then India, where I was a judge, then the Maldives, where I was also a judge, then China, then home, then Mali, then home again. Seventy-five thousand miles. On foot, mostly.
-> Why so far?
    Ibn Batootah: Everywhere I went there were people who read the same books and prayed the same prayers, from Morocco to the edge of China. A traveller could go anywhere and still be a guest. That is a world worth seeing.
-> Did you write it down?
    Ibn Batootah: When I got home, the sultan made me tell it all to a scribe. It is called The Gift to Those Who Contemplate the Wonders of Cities and the Marvels of Travelling. The short title was taken.
-> Goodbye.
    Ibn Batootah: Travel. It makes you speechless, then it makes you a storyteller.
===

title: history_zheng_hey
---
Zheng Hey: Hey! Welcome aboard. Well, welcome to the pier. My ship is over there. All of it. Do you see the end of it? Neither do I.
-> How big is your ship?
    Zheng Hey: Four hundred feet, some say. Nine masts. My fleet had three hundred ships and twenty-eight thousand sailors. We sailed to Arabia and East Africa and brought back a giraffe.
-> Why a giraffe?
    Zheng Hey: To show the emperor that the world respected him. The giraffe was a hit. Then a new emperor decided the fleets cost too much, burned the records, and China stopped sailing. Hey. What can you do.
-> What is the monsoon?
    Zheng Hey: The great wind of the Indian Ocean. It blows one way half the year and the other way the other half. Every trader from Kilwa to Calicut plans their whole life around it. Ride the dhow; it knows the season.
-> Goodbye.
    Zheng Hey: Hey! Bring back a giraffe!
===

title: history_vasco_da_gamer
---
Vasco da Gamer: Yo. Vasco da Gamer. Any-percent speedrun, Lisbon to India, around the bottom of Africa. Personal best: ten months.
-> Why go around Africa?
    Vasco da Gamer: The Ottomans held the shortcut and the Venetians held the prices. So Portugal found a skip. A very long skip. Half my crew died of scurvy. That is the bad part of speedrunning.
-> How did it go in India?
    Vasco da Gamer: I brought gifts: some hats and some honey. The king of Calicut was used to Indian Ocean merchants with gold and silk. He laughed at me. Then I came back with cannons. Not my best moment. Not anyone's.
-> Goodbye.
    Vasco da Gamer: GG. Mind the boom.
===

title: history_christopher_columbust
---
Christopher Columbust: Welcome to India!
-> This is not India.
    Christopher Columbust: It is India. I calculated it. The world is small and Asia is close. Everyone told me the world was bigger. Everyone was right, but I was lucky there was a continent in the way.
-> Didn't people already live here?
    Christopher Columbust: Millions of them, with cities and farms and empires. I did not discover anything. I arrived, and it went very badly for the people who were already here. That part is not funny, and I will not pretend it is.
-> What is the Columbian exchange?
    Christopher Columbust: Plants, animals and diseases crossing the ocean in both directions. Potatoes and maize fed Europe and China. Horses and cattle came west. Smallpox came west too, and that was the worst part of all of it.
-> Goodbye.
    Christopher Columbust: Next stop: also India!
===

title: history_captain_cookbook
---
Captain Cookbook: Captain James Cookbook, of His Majesty's Navy. Hungry? I have a recipe for that.
-> What is in the cookbook?
    Captain Cookbook: Sauerkraut. Pages of it. Sailors hated it, so I served it to the officers first. When the crew saw the officers eating it they demanded some. Nobody on my ships died of scurvy. Well, almost nobody.
-> Where did you sail?
    Captain Cookbook: The whole Pacific. Tahiti, New Zealand, the east coast of Australia, Hawaii. I mapped coasts that the people who lived there had known for a thousand years. The maps were new to us, not to them.
-> How did it end?
    Captain Cookbook: Badly, in Hawaii, in an argument over a stolen boat that I made much worse. Write down the maps. Write down the mistakes too.
-> Goodbye.
    Captain Cookbook: Eat your vegetables. It is an order.
===

title: history_adam_smithy
---
Adam Smithy: Good day! Adam Smithy, blacksmith and economist. This is my hand. And this is my invisible hand.
-> What does the invisible hand do?
    Adam Smithy: When everyone works for their own good, the market sometimes adds it up into the good of all. Sometimes. People forget the sometimes. I wrote a whole other book about sympathy and morals. Nobody quotes that one.
-> What is the division of labour?
    Adam Smithy: One man makes a whole pin, twenty pins a day. Ten men, each doing one step, make forty-eight thousand. Wonderful for pins. I did also say it was very bad for the men. They left that out too.
-> Goodbye.
    Adam Smithy: Buy low. Sell high. Read the whole book.
===

title: history_karl_marks
---
Karl Marks: You. Robot. Your work today: four out of ten. See me after class.
-> Why so low?
    Karl Marks: You do all the work and somebody else owns the factory. I give the factory owner a ten for the factory and you a four for the work. That is the problem. That is the whole problem. I wrote three volumes about it.
-> Were you right?
    Karl Marks: About how badly the factories treated people in my time, mostly yes. About what would come next, the twentieth century will have a lot of marking to do. I hear it does not go well. Not my department. My department is the marking.
-> Goodbye.
    Karl Marks: Workers of the world, hand in your homework.
===

title: history_general_ludd
---
General Ludd: Before you say anything: we are not against machines.
-> But you broke the looms.
    General Ludd: We broke the looms that the owners used to cut our pay and hire children to do bad work cheaply. We are weavers. We like looms. We built looms. We object to being starved by them.
-> What are you fighting for?
    General Ludd: Fair pay, decent work, and a say in how the new machines are used. The army was sent against us, and breaking a machine was made a hanging crime. And now our name means a fool who fears new things. History is unfair to the people who lose.
-> How do I open the factory gate?
    General Ludd: The boiler, in the mill. Stoke it and the engine runs, and the engine opens the gate. Everything here runs on that engine now. Even the gate.
-> Goodbye.
    General Ludd: When someone sells you a machine, ask who it is for.
===

title: history_lucy
---
Lucy: (She looks up at you, then at your feet. She stands up very straight, to show you she can.)
-> Hello.
    Lucy: (She tilts her head. She has seen a lot of visitors. Three point two million years of them, counting the scientists.)
-> Are you the first person?
    Mary Leaky: She is not a person like us. She is an Australopithecus. But she walked on two legs, like us, more than three million years ago. She is family. A very old cousin.
-> What is she doing here?
    Mary Leaky: Visiting. She lived three million years before anyone in this valley. Clio lets the old ones come to the atlas as guests, so you can meet them. Do not tell her she is early.
-> Why "Lucy"?
    Mary Leaky: The team that found her bones played a song all night in the camp. The song had a Lucy in it. In Ethiopia she has another name: Dinkinesh. It means "you are marvellous".
-> Goodbye.
    Lucy: (She waves. Or she reaches for a fig. It is hard to tell.)
===

title: history_mary_leaky
---
Mary Leaky: Mind the footprints. Mind the footprints! Thank you.
-> What are they?
    Mary Leaky: Laetoli. Two, maybe three people walked through wet volcanic ash three point six million years ago. The ash set like cement. They walked upright, just like you. Well, not like you. You are a robot.
-> Why is your bucket leaking?
    Mary Leaky: It is a dig bucket. They all leak. I have been brushing dirt in this valley for thirty years and I have never once had a dry boot.
-> How do you know how old things are?
    Mary Leaky: The layers. Ash falls, then soil, then ash. Each volcanic layer has a date in it, if you know how to read it. The ground is a book. The bottom pages are the oldest.
-> Goodbye.
    Mary Leaky: If you find a tooth, do not pick it up. Shout.
===

title: history_ochre_artist
---
Ochre Artist: Look. Look at this line, and this one, and how they cross.
-> What is it?
    Ochre Artist: A pattern. On ochre, the red stone. Nobody needs a pattern. That is why I made it. A thing you make when you do not need to is how you know a person was here.
-> Is it the first art?
    Ochre Artist: People keep finding older ones. Good. I hope they never stop. It means we were not the first to think of it.
-> What do you eat here?
    Ochre Artist: Shellfish. The sea is a larder that fills itself twice a day. People who live by the sea get clever. Ask anyone who has opened a mussel without a knife.
-> Goodbye.
    Ochre Artist: Take a bead. Shells, on a cord. Everyone who walks north takes one.
===

title: history_cattle_herder
---
Cattle Herder: Welcome to the lake. Yes, a lake. Yes, here. Do not tell me about the desert. It is not a desert yet.
-> The Sahara was green?
    Cattle Herder: Grass to the horizon, lakes full of fish and hippos, and my cows on all of it. The rains will move south one day. Then my grandchildren's grandchildren will move with them.
-> Who painted the rocks?
    Cattle Herder: We did. Cattle, mostly. People dancing. People with cattle. You paint what matters to you.
-> How do I cross the lake?
    Cattle Herder: The reed boat. It goes back and forth. Wait for it. Do not swim. The hippos are not friendly, whatever the paintings say.
-> Goodbye.
    Cattle Herder: The Crescent is up the ladder. We trade them cows. They trade us opinions about wheat.
===

title: history_kandake_can_do
---
Kandake Can-Do: Kneel or do not. Kush does not wait for you to decide.
-> Who are you?
    Kandake Can-Do: A kandake. A queen of Kush, up the Nile from Egypt. We ruled Egypt once, you know. The twenty-fifth dynasty. Egypt does not like to mention it.
-> Did you fight Rome?
    Kandake Can-Do: Rome came south. I lost an eye and kept the war. In the end Rome signed a treaty and stopped asking us for taxes. Rome does not like to mention that either.
-> Why are your pyramids so small?
    Kandake Can-Do: They are not small. They are steep. And there are more of them than in Egypt. Count them, if you have a week.
-> Goodbye.
    Kandake Can-Do: Can do. Will do. Did.
===

title: history_neville_neanderthal
---
Neville the Neanderthal: Oh, hello! Come in, come in, sit by the fire. Mind the nose. Everybody minds the nose.
-> Are you a caveman?
    Neville the Neanderthal: I live in a cave, I suppose. So do bats. I also make glue from birch tar, bury my friends with flowers and play the flute. Badly. But I play it.
-> What happened to the Neanderthals?
    Neville the Neanderthal: Some of us met your lot here, and got on rather well. Very well, in fact. If your ancestors left Africa, a little bit of you is me. You are welcome.
-> Were you stupid?
    Neville the Neanderthal: My brain is bigger than yours. I do not like to bring it up. But you brought it up.
-> Goodbye.
    Neville the Neanderthal: Off to Europe? Take a coat. Take two coats.
===

title: history_enheduanna
---
Enheduanna: Read this. No, out loud. Yes. That is mine.
-> Who are you?
    Enheduanna: High priestess of the moon at Ur, daughter of Sargon, and the first author whose name anybody knows. Before me there were stories. After me there were writers.
-> What did you write?
    Enheduanna: Hymns, to the goddess Inanna. Forty-two of them for the temples. I wrote that I was writing them, too. Nobody had thought to do that before.
-> What is it like in Uruk?
    Enheduanna: The first real city. Walls, temples, crowds, beer rations, and more paperwork than anybody can bear. It is wonderful. It is going to fall to pieces eventually. Everything here does.
-> Goodbye.
    Enheduanna: Sign your work. Somebody in four thousand years will want to know.
===

title: history_cave_painter
---
Cave Painter: Hold the lamp up. Higher. There. Now look at the horse.
-> It moves.
    Cave Painter: In the flicker, yes. I paint them on the bumps of the rock, so the rock gives them muscles. The lamp does the rest.
-> Whose hands are those?
    Cave Painter: Ours. You put your hand on the wall and blow the paint around it. Some are children. Some are mine. It means "I was here", and it says it better than words.
-> Why paint in the dark?
    Cave Painter: People ask that for forty thousand years. I do not know what to tell them. Where else would you keep something important?
-> Goodbye.
    Cave Painter: Take the lamp out with you. The cave does not need it. The horses can see in the dark.
===

title: history_otzi_nice_man
---
Ötzi the Nice-man: Hello! Lovely day. Bit cold. I spent five thousand years in a glacier, so I have a low bar.
-> What happened to you?
    Ötzi the Nice-man: Arrow in the back, up on the pass. Somebody did not like me. I was very nice. I have no idea why. It is the oldest cold case in Europe.
-> What are the tattoos?
    Ötzi the Nice-man: Sixty-one little lines, on my sore joints. Medicine, probably. I had bad knees. You walk over the Alps in grass shoes and see how your knees are.
-> What are you carrying?
    Ötzi the Nice-man: A copper axe, a bow I had not finished, a pouch of mushrooms for a fire, a kit for repairs. I was ready for everything except the arrow.
-> Goodbye.
    Ötzi the Nice-man: Stay warm! Stay out of glaciers! It worked out for me in the end, but I do not recommend it.
===

title: history_not_a_druid
---
Not-a-Druid: Ah. You have come to admire my stones.
-> Did you build them?
    Not-a-Druid: Yes. Well. My people. Well. The stones were here when my people got here. About two thousand years before. But I have looked after them very carefully since.
-> Then who built them?
    Not-a-Druid: Farmers, with ropes and sledges and a great deal of beer. They hauled some of the stones from Wales. Wales! I cannot get my neighbour to haul a log.
-> What are they for?
    Not-a-Druid: Midsummer sunrise comes up over the heel stone. Midwinter sunset goes down between the big ones. It is a calendar, a temple and a party, all at once. Pull the sun stone and see.
-> Goodbye.
    Not-a-Druid: Tell everyone I built it. They will believe you. They always do.
===

title: history_boudic_car
---
Boudic-car: Get in. We are going to Londinium.
-> Who are you?
    Boudic-car: Queen of the Iceni. Rome took my kingdom when my husband died and treated my family worse. So I took three Roman towns. That is how sums work.
-> What happened?
    Boudic-car: We burned Londinium to the ground. There is still a red layer of ash under the city. Then the legions met us on a field of their own choosing. The Romans wrote the story. They always write the story.
-> Why the chariot?
    Boudic-car: Speed. Noise. Fear. Also there are no knives on the wheels, whatever the statues say. Those would cut my own horses.
-> Goodbye.
    Boudic-car: Rome is down the ladder. Tell them I am coming. They know.
===

title: history_leif_errandson
---
Leif Errandson: Ho! You look like someone who has been somewhere. So have I. Everywhere, nearly.
-> Where have you been?
    Leif Errandson: West of Greenland, there is a land with grapes and big trees. I called it Vinland. I built some houses, had a look round, and came home. The Spaniards will make a big fuss about it five hundred years later.
-> Why did you leave?
    Leif Errandson: The people who already lived there did not want us. Fair enough. Also it was a long way to go for timber.
-> Were the Vikings just raiders?
    Leif Errandson: Some of us raided. Most of us farmed, fished, traded and argued about law in a field every summer. Raiding is what people write about. Nobody writes a song about a good barley year.
-> Goodbye.
    Leif Errandson: And no horns on the helmet. Who would wear horns into a fight? Think about it.
===

title: history_coast_walker
---
Coast Walker: Keep the sea on your right and you will not get lost. That is all the map we have.
-> Where are you going?
    Coast Walker: East. Along the beach. Then the next beach. Each one is only a little further than the last. My great-grandmother started this. My great-granddaughter will finish it, somewhere I will never see.
-> How fast is the walk?
    Coast Walker: A few miles a generation. Slow, for one person. But from Africa to Australia in a few thousand years. Fast, for everyone.
-> Is it dangerous?
    Coast Walker: Tigers. Crabs that pinch. Tides that do not wait. Also, it is beautiful. Both are true.
-> Goodbye.
    Coast Walker: Up the ladder is the sea crossing. Down is the long way round to the cold places. Choose the warm way.
===

title: history_dentist_of_mehrgarh
---
Dentist of Mehrgarh: Open wide. Wider. Hm. Hm. That will need a drill.
-> You have a drill?
    Dentist of Mehrgarh: A bow drill with a flint tip. I drill a hole in the bad tooth, and the pain goes. Mostly. It is nine thousand years before anaesthetic. Bite this stick.
-> Why do people need a dentist?
    Dentist of Mehrgarh: Farming. Foragers had good teeth. Now everyone eats barley porridge all day and their teeth fall to pieces. Every new thing makes a new job.
-> What else is in Mehrgarh?
    Dentist of Mehrgarh: Barley, wheat, cotton, cattle and mud-brick houses. The village grows and grows. One day there will be great cities down the river. They will need dentists too.
-> Goodbye.
    Dentist of Mehrgarh: Rinse. Spit. Come back in six months. Or nine thousand years.
===

title: history_priest_king_probably
---
The Priest-King (Probably): Greetings. I am the Priest-King. Probably.
-> Probably?
    The Priest-King (Probably): Somebody made a statue of me and called it "Priest-King". Nobody can read our writing, so nobody knows who I am. Including me. I have decided to believe the label.
-> What is this city?
    The Priest-King (Probably): Mohenjo-daro. Straight streets, baked bricks all the same size, a covered drain under every street and a bathroom in every house. Nobody else in the world has drains this good. Nobody will, for thousands of years.
-> Who rules here?
    The Priest-King (Probably): That is the odd thing. We find no great palaces and no big tombs of kings. Maybe nobody ruled very much. Maybe we just agreed about bricks.
-> How do I get into the citadel?
    The Priest-King (Probably): Show the seal. The one with the unicorn. It is on the granary roof. Everything important is on the granary roof.
-> Goodbye.
    The Priest-King (Probably): If you ever read our script, tell me what it says. I have wondered.
===

title: history_raft_builder
---
Raft Builder: Hold this. Now this. Now tie them. Good. You are a raft builder now.
-> Where are you going?
    Raft Builder: Over there. You cannot see it. That is the point. The smoke of fires on the other side tells us there is land. So we go.
-> Is it far?
    Raft Builder: Out of sight of land, for days. Sixty-five thousand years ago, this is the longest sea crossing anybody has ever made. Somebody has to be first. Today it is my cousin.
-> What is on the other side?
    Raft Builder: Sahul. The land that will be Australia and New Guinea, one land while the sea is low. Big animals. Very big. My people will live there longer than anyone has lived anywhere, and look after it the whole time.
-> Goodbye.
    Raft Builder: Ride the raft over the strait. Do not stand up. Never stand up on a raft.
===

title: history_kuk_gardener
---
Kuk Gardener: Mind the ditch. We dug them all by hand, to drain the swamp.
-> What are you growing?
    Kuk Gardener: Taro, bananas, sugar cane, yams. We started gardening here nearly ten thousand years ago. On our own. Nobody taught us.
-> On your own?
    Kuk Gardener: People think farming was invented once and spread. No. In the Crescent, in China, in the Americas, in Africa, and here, people worked it out on their own. Clever people are everywhere.
-> What are those birds?
    Kuk Gardener: Cassowaries. Very big. Very fast. One kick from them and you will have a long rest. Walk round them.
-> Goodbye.
    Kuk Gardener: Take a banana. Ours was one of the first. You can thank us whenever you like.
===

title: history_denny_denisovan
---
Denny the Denisovan: Have you seen my finger? Not this one. The famous one.
-> Your famous finger?
    Denny the Denisovan: Scientists found a little finger bone in a cave in Siberia. They read the DNA and found a whole new kind of people. Us! From one finger! Plus some teeth. And half a jaw, later. We are the best-known people that nobody has ever seen.
-> What are Denisovans like?
    Denny the Denisovan: Strong. Good in the cold and very good in the mountains. If your family is from Tibet, you can breathe thin air thanks to us. If your family is from New Guinea, you carry a lot of us too. You are welcome.
-> Goodbye.
    Denny the Denisovan: If you find the rest of me, bring it back. I would like to know what my face looks like.
===

title: history_leizu
---
Leizu: Careful. That is my tea.
-> What is in it?
    Leizu: A cocoon. It fell in from the mulberry tree. When I took it out, it unwound into one long, fine thread. That is silk. That is how it started, the story says.
-> Is that true?
    Leizu: It is a story. The silk is true. People along this river have kept silk worms for five thousand years. For a very long time it was a secret, and anyone who took the worms out of China was in very big trouble.
-> What grows here?
    Leizu: Millet in the north, rice in the south, mulberries for the worms. The yellow river floods, and moves, and floods again. They call it China's sorrow. It is also the reason everyone lives here.
-> Goodbye.
    Leizu: Mind the moths. They are very proud of themselves.
===

title: history_fu_hao
---
Lady Fu Hao: Stand straight. You are in the presence of a general.
-> Who are you?
    Lady Fu Hao: A queen of the Shang king, and the commander of his armies. Thirteen thousand soldiers. I won every war he sent me to. He sent me to many.
-> What are the bones for?
    Lady Fu Hao: We ask the ancestors questions. Will it rain? Will the king's tooth stop hurting? Will Fu Hao win? We carve the question in a bone, heat it, and read the cracks. Then we write down the answer. It is the oldest Chinese writing there is.
-> How do I open the bronze gate?
    Lady Fu Hao: Ask the ancestors. Crack the oracle bone. They usually say yes to visitors. They are very old, and they like company.
-> Goodbye.
    Lady Fu Hao: My tomb has a hundred and thirty bronze weapons in it. I liked to be ready.
===

title: history_dr_clovis_first
---
Dr. Clovis-First: Stop right there. This is the first site in the Americas. Nobody was here before Clovis.
-> Are you sure?
    Dr. Clovis-First: Absolutely. Thirteen thousand years ago, the first people came over the land bridge, through the ice, and made these fine fluted points. Nothing is older.
-> Somebody found an older site.
    Dr. Clovis-First: A mistake. Bad dating. Contamination.
    -> Somebody found another one.
        Dr. Clovis-First: ...a coincidence.
    -> And footprints in New Mexico, twenty-three thousand years old.
        Dr. Clovis-First: I am not listening. I have a pith helmet and a theory, and I am keeping both.
-> Who really came first?
    Dr. Clovis-First: The people who came first. Their great-grandchildren are still here. Ask them. That is more than an archaeologist can tell you.
-> Goodbye.
    Dr. Clovis-First: First! Clovis! First! ...Probably.
===

title: history_teosinte_breeder
---
Teosinte Breeder: Look at this. A teosinte ear. It is the size of my thumb, with a few hard seeds. And this is what it turns into.
-> That is corn?
    Teosinte Breeder: Maize. It took my people thousands of years. Every year, keep the seeds of the best plant. Plant them. Keep the best again. No plant has ever changed so much because people looked after it.
-> Was farming invented here?
    Teosinte Breeder: Yes, here, on our own. Maize, beans, squash, chilli, cacao. Up the mountains, potatoes, on the terraces. Half the food in the world comes from the Americas. Tomatoes, too. Tell Italy.
-> What are the llamas for?
    Teosinte Breeder: Wool, carrying, and judging you. They are very good at the last one.
-> Goodbye.
    Teosinte Breeder: Plant the best seed. Always the best seed. Your grandchildren will thank you.
===

title: history_head_sculptor
---
Head Sculptor: Stand back a little. Basalt dust gets everywhere.
-> Who is the head?
    Head Sculptor: A ruler. Each head is one person, with his own face and his own helmet. When he is gone, the stone still looks out.
-> How did you move it?
    Head Sculptor: The basalt comes from the mountains, a long way off. No wheels, no oxen. Rafts on the rivers, and many, many people pulling. Then I start with a hammerstone and a lot of patience.
-> What are the black balls?
    Head Sculptor: Rubber. Cook the milk of the rubber tree with the juice of the morning glory, and it bounces. We were doing this three thousand years before anyone else. The people who came later called us the rubber people.
-> Goodbye.
    Head Sculptor: Try the bouncy part by the trees. Everybody does.
===

title: history_zero_hero
---
The Zero Hero: Count with me. A dot is one. A bar is five. And this shell is...
-> Nothing?
    The Zero Hero: Zero! Not nothing. A number for nothing, in its own place. Without it you cannot write twenty, or four hundred, or the date. We use it in the Long Count, every day, and nobody in Europe will have one for a thousand years.
-> What is the Long Count?
    The Zero Hero: Our calendar counts days from the start of this world. Every stela says exactly which day it was put up. Archaeologists love us. We even wrote down eclipses ahead of time, in books of bark paper.
-> What happened to the Maya?
    The Zero Hero: Some of the great cities emptied, after droughts and wars. The Maya did not. Millions of us still speak Maya languages. Ask them where we went. They will say: nowhere.
-> Goodbye.
    The Zero Hero: Remember the shell. Without it, you are nothing. With it, you are zero, which is better.
===

title: history_lady_six_sky_high
---
Lady Six Sky-High: Kneel. No? Very well, stand. I am Lady Six Sky-High, of Naranjo. I came here as a princess and I stayed as the ruler.
-> You ruled the city?
    Lady Six Sky-High: For decades. My son was the king, technically. He was five. Somebody had to fight the wars, put up the monuments and be painted standing on a captive. That was me.
-> What is the ballcourt for?
    Lady Six Sky-High: The game. A rubber ball, hips only, no hands. Get it through the stone ring and the gate opens. It is the hardest shot in the world. I have never missed. Nobody has ever been allowed to see me try.
-> Goodbye.
    Lady Six Sky-High: Look up when you leave. My name is on that stela. Twice.
===

title: history_cacica_of_boriken
---
Cacica of Boriken: Welcome to Boriken. You are a guest of my yucayeque, my village. Sit in the shade of the bohio.
-> You are the chief?
    Cacica of Boriken: Cacica. Among the Taino a woman can lead, and I do. I speak for the village, I host the areito, our songs and dances of our history, and I settle the quarrels about the batey.
-> Where is Boriken?
    Cacica of Boriken: Here! You will hear it called Puerto Rico, "rich port": the Spanish name of a harbour town that, in time, became the name of the whole island. Its people still call themselves boricua. That island to the west is Ayiti, land of mountains, which you call Haiti and the Dominican Republic. Cuba is Cuba; that name is ours too. And south, Xaymaca, land of wood and water. You say Jamaica.
-> What is a batey?
    Cacica of Boriken: The plaza and the ball court, edged with carved stones. Two teams, one rubber ball, and no hands. The ball is older than any of us. It came up the islands from the land in the west.
-> What do you eat?
    Cacica of Boriken: Casabe, bread of cassava. Grate the root, squeeze out the bitter juice, bake it flat on the buren. It keeps for months, so a canoe can carry it far. Fish, from the barbacoa. Fruit, from everywhere.
-> What happens next?
    Cacica of Boriken: In 1492 strangers will come to these islands. Within a generation most of my people will die of their diseases, their wars and their forced labour. Not all. Our children's children are still in Boriken, in Ayiti, in Cuba. Our words are in your mouth. We are still here.
-> Goodbye.
    Cacica of Boriken: Go well. Take casabe for the road.
===

title: history_canoe_maker
---
Canoe Maker: One tree. One canoe. Many, many days with the stone axe and the fire.
-> How big are they?
    Canoe Maker: The big ones carry a hundred paddlers. We go from island to island to trade, to visit, to play the ball game against the next village. The sea is our road.
-> You said canoe.
    Canoe Maker: Canoa. Your word. You have a lot of ours: hammock, from hamaca, for sleeping off the ground. Hurricane, from hurakan, the great storm. Barbecue, from barbacoa. Savanna, maize, tobacco. You are welcome.
-> Can I ride one?
    Canoe Maker: Step in the middle and do not stand up. The sea between the islands is wider than it looks.
-> Goodbye.
    Canoe Maker: Sit in the hammock before you go. It throws you up to the ceiba. That is what hammocks are for. Mostly.
===

title: history_lapita_potter
---
Lapita Potter: Mind the pots. Every one is stamped with this, a little toothed comb. Line after line, face after face.
-> Why stamp them?
    Lapita Potter: So they are ours. Find a pot like this on any beach from the Bismarck islands to Samoa, and you know we came by. We go where nobody has been, and we leave our pots.
-> Where nobody has been?
    Lapita Potter: The islands out there have never had people. We are the first to sail out of sight of land and keep going: to Vanuatu, to Fiji, to Tonga, to Samoa. Our grandparents' grandparents came from far to the north and west, from Taiwan.
-> What do you bring?
    Lapita Potter: Pigs, dogs, chickens, taro, breadfruit, and pots. Everything a village needs, in a canoe. We also bring the rats, but nobody invited them.
-> Goodbye.
    Lapita Potter: Take the outrigger across the lagoon. The house on stilts is mine. Do not touch the pots.
===

title: history_wayfinder
---
Wayfinder: Quiet a moment. Feel the swell under your feet? It comes from the south-east. It has been coming from there for three days.
-> How do you find your way?
    Wayfinder: The stars rise and set at the same places on the horizon all night. I know each one's house. When it is cloudy, the swells. When I am close, the birds: at dusk the terns fly home to land, and I follow them. And the clouds stand still over an island.
-> No compass? No map?
    Wayfinder: The compass is in my head. The map is in my head. My teachers put them there, and their teachers put them in theirs. We found every island in the Pacific that way: Hawaii, Aotearoa, Rapa Nui. Thousands of miles, on purpose, there and back.
-> What are the big birds?
    Wayfinder: Frigatebirds. Pirates of the sky. They take the fish out of other birds' mouths. If one comes at you, duck.
-> Goodbye.
    Wayfinder: Board the canoe. Sit on the deck between the hulls. I will tell you when we are there.
===

title: history_moai_carver
---
Moai Carver: This one is for my grandfather. He will stand on the ahu with his back to the sea and look after the village.
-> How do you move them?
    Moai Carver: They walk. Ropes on both sides and one at the back. Rock him left, rock him right, and he walks down the road from the quarry. The stories always said so, and now the scientists have tried it too.
-> What is on the tablet?
    Moai Carver: Rongorongo. Writing. Rows of signs, and every second row upside down so you turn the board as you read. Nobody can read it now. Somebody will, one day.
-> Did your people ruin the island?
    Moai Carver: That is a story about us that outsiders like to tell. The rats ate the palm seeds, not us. We farmed the island well for hundreds of years. In 1862 slave ships took about fifteen hundred of us, and the sickness after took most of the rest. That is what happened.
-> Goodbye.
    Moai Carver: Ride my grandfather over the ditch, if you like. Gently. He is new.
===

title: history_thor_hey_im_right_dahl
---
Thor Hey-I'm-Right-Dahl: Ahoy! In 1947 I sailed a balsa raft from Peru to Polynesia. One hundred and one days! Proof that Polynesia was settled from South America!
-> Was it?
    Thor Hey-I'm-Right-Dahl: Obviously. Well. The languages come from the west. The pots come from the west. The DNA comes from the west. The chickens, the pigs and the canoes come from the west. But apart from that, obviously.
-> So you were wrong?
    Thor Hey-I'm-Right-Dahl: I was a little right! The sweet potato came from South America. And some Polynesians have South American ancestors, from around 1200 CE. So someone went there and came back. It just was not my raft, and it was not that way round.
-> Who went?
    Thor Hey-I'm-Right-Dahl: The Polynesians, probably. Into the wind, on purpose, and home again. Rather better sailors than a man on a raft who drifted. Do not tell my publisher.
-> Goodbye.
    Thor Hey-I'm-Right-Dahl: Kon-Tiki! Still a great book! Mostly wrong! Great book!
===

title: history_brass_caster
---
Brass Caster: Stand back from the furnace. The brass in that crucible is hotter than anything you have ever touched, and it will be a plaque for the Oba by tonight.
-> How do you make it?
    Brass Caster: Lost wax. I shape the figure in beeswax over a core of clay. I coat it in more clay. I heat it, and the wax runs out: that is the "lost". Into the empty space I pour the brass. Then I break the mould. Every piece is the only one.
-> Who do you make it for?
    Brass Caster: The Oba. Only our guild, the Igun Eronmwon, may cast brass in Benin, and only for the court. We live on Igun Street. Our grandchildren's grandchildren will still be casting on Igun Street. You can go and watch them.
-> Where does the brass come from?
    Brass Caster: Those rings. Manillas. The Portuguese ships bring them by the thousand, made far away in Europe, and trade them for pepper, ivory, cloth, and people. We melt them down and make history out of them.
-> Pull the bellows?
    Brass Caster: Go on, work them. When the plaque is cast, the guild carries it to the palace, and the palace gate opens for us. It always opens for the brass.
-> Goodbye.
    Brass Caster: Mind the ants on the road. They march for no Oba.
===

title: history_iyoba_idia
---
Iyoba Idia: You stand in the court of Benin. I am Idia, mother of the Oba Esigie.
-> What is an Iyoba?
    Iyoba Idia: The queen mother. There was none before me. When the Igala of Idah came against my son, I raised an army of my own and went with it to war. We won. My son made the title for me, and gave me my own palace at Uselu.
-> You went to war?
    Iyoba Idia: With counsel, with medicine and with soldiers. A mother does not wait at home to hear how her son's war went.
-> Those brass plaques...
    Iyoba Idia: The history of this court, in brass, nailed to the pillars: who fought, who ruled, who came to trade. The Oba wears my face in ivory, on his belt, at the great ceremonies. So I am in the history too.
-> What happens next?
    Iyoba Idia: In 1897 a British force will come, burn this city, send the Oba Ovonramwen into exile and carry off the brass and ivory of the court, thousands of pieces, my face among them. When Nigeria asks to borrow my face back for a festival in 1977, the museum will say it is too fragile to travel. It travelled here, did it not?
-> Goodbye.
    Iyoba Idia: Go with the blessing of the court. And walk around the ants.
===

title: history_lord_finders_keepers
---
Lord Finders-Keepers: Ah! A visitor! Don't touch the glass. These are being kept safe.
-> Safe from whom?
    Lord Finders-Keepers: From... well, from the people who made them, I suppose. It sounds worse when you say it out loud.
-> How did you get them?
    Lord Finders-Keepers: 1897. A punitive expedition. We punished the city by burning it, and we punished the art by taking it home. Thousands of pieces. Auctioned some to pay for the trip. Very efficient.
-> What does the card say?
    Lord Finders-Keepers: "ON LOAN". From Benin. We simply have not decided when the loan ends. These things take time. About a hundred and twenty years, so far.
-> Give them back.
    Lord Finders-Keepers: Out of the question! Well. Germany gave back more than a thousand. A college in Cambridge gave back a brass cockerel. A museum in London gave back seventy-two. Others are thinking about it very hard. I am thinking about it extremely slowly.
-> Goodbye.
    Lord Finders-Keepers: Finders keepers! That is the law. Well, it is a saying. Well, it is a thing children say.
===

title: history_toussaint_louverture
---
Toussaint Louverture: Welcome to Saint-Domingue. Soon it will have its own name again.
-> Who are you?
    Toussaint Louverture: I was born enslaved on the Breda plantation, near Cap-Francais. I tended horses, I learned to read, and I was free before the rising. In 1791 I joined it. I learned war the way I learned to read: one page at a time.
-> How did you win?
    Toussaint Louverture: Spain, Britain and France all wanted this island. I fought for whichever side would end slavery, and in 1794 France did. Then we drove out the British. By 1801 I governed the whole island.
-> What happens to you?
    Toussaint Louverture: In 1802 Napoleon's general asks me to a meeting, and arrests me there. I will die in a cold fort in the mountains of France, in April 1803. As they take me on board the ship, I will tell them: in overthrowing me, you have cut down only the trunk of the tree of liberty. It will grow again from the roots, for they are many and deep.
-> Were you right?
    Toussaint Louverture: Walk east and see. Mind the soldiers in the mountains. They are not used to them. We are.
-> Goodbye.
    Toussaint Louverture: Go well, and read everything you can.
===

title: history_catherine_flon
---
Catherine Flon: Careful, the seam is still fresh. This is the flag.
-> What happened?
    Catherine Flon: At Arcahaie, in May 1803, the generals met to make one army of all of us. General Dessalines took the French flag and tore the white out of it. The blue and the red were left. He gave them to me, and I sewed them together.
-> Why the white?
    Catherine Flon: You can read it the way you like. The French flag, without France in it. The blue and red of the people who were left to fight. Our cry was: Liberty or Death.
-> Is that what really happened?
    Catherine Flon: That is the story Haiti tells, and I am in it. Every eighteenth of May, Haiti keeps Flag Day, and the schoolchildren learn my name.
-> Goodbye.
    Catherine Flon: Go on east. On the first day of the year, there will be soup.
===

title: history_soup_joumou_cook
---
Soup Joumou Cook: Sit, sit. First day of the year, first day of Haiti. Everybody eats.
-> What is it?
    Soup Joumou Cook: Soup joumou. Squash, beef, cabbage, carrots, a little hot pepper, all day in the pot. Before, we cooked it for the masters, and we were not allowed to eat it. Today, the first of January 1804, we all eat it.
-> What happened today?
    Soup Joumou Cook: At Gonaives, General Dessalines proclaimed the Act of Independence. Saint-Domingue is gone. This island is Haiti again, the name the Taino gave it: Ayiti, land of mountains. The first country made by people who freed themselves.
-> And after?
    Soup Joumou Cook: In 1825, French warships will come and demand money: 150 million francs, for the "property" France lost. That property was us. Haiti will pay it, and the loans for it, until 1947. The United States will not even recognise us until 1862.
-> Do you still eat it?
    Soup Joumou Cook: Every first of January, in every Haitian home in the world. In 2021 UNESCO put it on its list of the heritage of humanity. Have another bowl.
-> Goodbye.
    Soup Joumou Cook: Take some for the road. It is better the second day.
===

title: history_ottobah_cugoano
---
Ottobah Cugoano: My name is Ottobah Cugoano. I am Fante, from this coast. I was thirteen when I was brought to a place like this.
-> What happened?
    Ottobah Cugoano: I was playing with other children near my home, in 1770. Men I did not fear took us away. On the coast they sold me, and I was shut in a fort like this one, and then put on a ship. I was taken to Grenada, to the plantations.
-> Who sold you?
    Ottobah Cugoano: I will not hide it: men of my own colour kidnapped me and sold me. But if there were no buyers, there would be no sellers. The trade was made by the Europeans who bought, and their laws that allowed it.
-> How did you get out?
    Ottobah Cugoano: A master took me to England in 1772. There I learned to read and write, and I was free. I used it. With friends I helped free a man who had been kidnapped onto a ship in the Thames. We called ourselves the Sons of Africa.
-> Your book?
    Ottobah Cugoano: Thoughts and Sentiments on the Evil and Wicked Traffic of the Slavery and Commerce of the Human Species. London, 1787. It says that the trade must end, and that every enslaved person must be freed, now. Not slowly. Now.
-> Goodbye.
    Ottobah Cugoano: Read it. And remember that every number on the guide's papers was a person with a name.
===

title: history_castle_guide
---
Castle Guide: Welcome. Please walk slowly here. My family has fished from the beach outside these walls for as long as anyone remembers. I tell visitors what the walls held.
-> What was this place?
    Castle Guide: A trading castle of the European companies. They came for gold, and the coast was called the Gold Coast. Then the trade that paid most was in people. Upstairs the governors lived, with a view of the sea. Below, the dungeons.
-> The dungeon?
    Castle Guide: Hundreds of people at a time, men in one dungeon, women in another, for weeks or months, in the dark, until a ship came. In some castles on this coast, the church was built right over the men's dungeon.
-> How many?
    Castle Guide: From 1501 to 1866, about twelve and a half million people were forced onto ships for the Americas. About ten point seven million lived to land. They resisted: about one voyage in ten had a revolt on board.
-> And the door?
    Castle Guide: The last place they stood in Africa. It goes one way. If you go through, you come out on the far side of the ocean, in Saint-Domingue. Today the sea side of a door like this has a second sign: the Door of Return. Their descendants come back through it as guests.
-> Goodbye.
    Castle Guide: Thank you for walking slowly.
===

title: history_potato_farmer
---
Potato Farmer: Mind the rows. Every one of these is a different potato, and I know them all by name.
-> Where did potatoes come from?
    Potato Farmer: From here. The wild ones are small and bitter, and some will make you sick. Our grandmothers' grandmothers kept the ones that did not, year after year, until the potato was this. When a bitter one is all we have, we eat it with a sauce of clay. The clay takes the poison.
-> What is on the high plain?
    Potato Farmer: Chuno. Up there it freezes every night and the sun is hard every day. We lay the potatoes out, let them freeze, let them thaw, and stamp the water out with our feet. Then they keep for years. A bad harvest does not kill us.
-> And those animals?
    Potato Farmer: Guanacos, wild and rude, and they spit. One day our children's children will tame their cousins into llamas and alpacas, and then we will have wool, and something to carry the potatoes. Today, I carry the potatoes.
-> What happens next?
    Potato Farmer: In a few thousand years, men from Spain will come, take the silver, and take a few potatoes as an afterthought. Some will say the potatoes were worth more than all the silver. They may be right.
-> Goodbye.
    Potato Farmer: Take one. Take a purple one. You have never had a purple one.
===

title: history_antoine_parmen_tater
---
Antoine Parmen-tater: Antoine Parmen-tater, pharmacist, of Paris! I am the man who taught France to love the potato. Bon appe-tater!
-> You taught France?
    Antoine Parmen-tater: I was a prisoner of war in Prussia, and they fed me nothing but potatoes. I lived! Back in France, people said potatoes caused leprosy. So I made it my life's work to prove them wrong. With science. And with marketing.
-> What marketing?
    Antoine Parmen-tater: I planted a field of potatoes outside Paris and put soldiers round it all day. At night I sent the soldiers home. The people thought: anything guarded must be precious. They stole every plant. Some historians say this story is too good to be true. I say it is exactly as good as it needs to be.
-> The flowers?
    Antoine Parmen-tater: I gave the King a bouquet of potato flowers, and he wore one in his buttonhole. The court copied him. Purple is very fashionable when the King does it.
-> But people farmed them here for thousands of years.
    Antoine Parmen-tater: Yes, yes, for eight thousand years, before anyone in Paris had heard of them. I did not invent the potato. I merely gave it a French accent. Now every potato and minced-beef pie in France is named after me: hachis Parmentier. Look it up. Mash it up.
-> Goodbye.
    Antoine Parmen-tater: Au revoir! Remember: a potato a day keeps the famine away. Mostly. Do not plant only one kind.
===

title: history_caral_flautist
---
Caral Flautist: Welcome to Caral! Mind the steps. There are a lot of them, and I carried some.
-> How old is Caral?
    Caral Flautist: Old. While the Egyptians stack their first pyramids of stone, we are stacking ours. Nobody in Egypt has heard of us, and nobody here has heard of them, and we are both very proud.
-> What is the flute made of?
    Caral Flautist: A pelican's wing bone. In one of our plazas you will find thirty-two flutes, of pelican and of condor bone. We were not a quiet city.
-> Where are your walls?
    Caral Flautist: We have none, and no weapons of war. People come here to trade, to build and to play music. That is how it looks from up here, and from down in the digging, so far.
-> And that knotted cord?
    Caral Flautist: Records, maybe. Counts of cotton, of fish, of debts. If I told you what it says, the archaeologists would be out of a job.
-> What happens next?
    Caral Flautist: One day, about 1800 BCE, people will leave Caral. A drought, maybe; the sea changed, maybe. Nobody is sure. The pyramids will wait under the sand for four thousand years for somebody to look.
-> Goodbye.
    Caral Flautist: Come back when the plaza is full. It is louder then.
===

title: history_net_maker
---
Net Maker of Aspero: From the coast, are you? No? You smell of mountains.
-> What do you trade?
    Net Maker of Aspero: Up the valley they grow the cotton. Down here we make it into nets. The sea fills the nets with anchovies, and the anchovies go up the valley. Everybody wins except the anchovies.
-> The Supe valley?
    Net Maker of Aspero: Supe. Yes, like soup. It is mostly anchovy soup, so the name is fair.
-> Do you cook in pots?
    Net Maker of Aspero: Pots? Nobody here makes pots yet. We have gourds. A gourd is a pot that grows on a vine. Why would you bake one?
-> Who fed the first cities?
    Net Maker of Aspero: Some archaeologists say the sea fed the first cities of the Andes, before the farms could. I say: obviously. Others say the farms. They have not tasted my anchovies.
-> Goodbye.
    Net Maker of Aspero: Take a net. You will catch something. Possibly a fish.
===

title: history_pig_painter
---
Pig Painter: Stand back from the wall. Not that far. There. Now you can see the pig.
-> What is it doing?
    Pig Painter: Standing. The three beside it are people, or something like people, holding things out to it. A rope, a spear. Maybe it is a hunt. Maybe it is a story about a hunt. You will have to guess. That is what a story is for.
-> How do you paint in the dark?
    Pig Painter: With a fire, and red earth ground in water. For the hands, put yours on the rock, fill your mouth with paint and blow. Afterwards the hand is gone and the rock remembers it.
-> Is that a real pig?
    Pig Painter: A warty pig. They live in the forest here, and only on these islands. Your children's children will still see them. Mind the tusks.
-> How did you get here?
    Pig Painter: By sea. Everyone did. These islands never touched the lands to the west or the lands to the south, not even when the sea was low. Our grandmothers built rafts and went where they could see smoke, or birds, or nothing at all.
-> Goodbye.
    Pig Painter: Leave your hand on the wall if you like. Fifty thousand years is a long time to be remembered.
===

title: history_word_keeper
---
Word Keeper: Sit, sit. Mind the pot. Do you know what this is? Mata. Eye. Point at your eye. Mata.
-> Why do you keep words?
    Word Keeper: Because our words travel further than we do. My grandmother's grandmother came south from the big island you call Taiwan. Her words came with her. They go on ahead now, from island to island, in the canoes.
-> How far?
    Word Keeper: Further than anyone will believe. One day mata will mean eye in Malay, in Tagalog, in Fiji and in Aotearoa. On Madagascar, by Africa, it will be maso; in Hawaii, in the middle of the great sea, maka. The same word, worn smooth by the road. Lima, five, the fingers of one hand: Malay, Tagalog, Fijian. In Hawaii lima is five and the hand itself. Half the world, by canoe.
-> You speak my language?
    Word Keeper: You speak a little of ours. Tattoo, from tatau. Taboo, from tapu. Those came the long way round, through the far islands. Keep them; they suit you.
-> What do you bring in the canoes?
    Word Keeper: Rice and millet, pigs and dogs, red-slipped pots like this one, and the outrigger, which keeps a canoe the right way up. On the new islands we find bananas, taro and sugarcane, and we take those too.
-> Goodbye.
    Word Keeper: Selamat jalan, they will say in Malay: go safely. Paalam, in Tagalog. And from me: mind the pot.
===

title: history_trung_trac
---
Trưng Trắc: Stranger, you stand in Mê Linh. My sister and I hold it, and sixty-five citadels besides. The Han governor has run home.
-> Who are you?
    Trưng Trắc: Trưng Trắc, daughter of the Lạc lord of Mê Linh. That is my sister, Trưng Nhị. In the year you call 40 CE we raised the Lạc lords against Han rule, and the people rose with us.
-> Why did you rise?
    Trưng Trắc: The governor, Tô Định, took our land for his taxes and put our ways under his law. The stories say he killed my husband, Thi Sách; others say not. Either way, I did not need a husband's death to want my country back.
-> What happens next?
    Trưng Trắc: Three years we rule. Then the Han send their best general, Ma Yuan, with a great army, and in 43 CE we lose. The Han chronicles say they took our heads. Our people say we went into the river rather than be taken. Choose which story you believe. Both say we did not kneel.
-> Are you remembered?
    Trưng Trắc: Ask anyone in Vietnam. There are temples to the Two Ladies Trưng, a street named for us in every town, and a day of the year that is ours.
-> Goodbye.
    Trưng Trắc: Go. If anyone asks who rules here, say: we do.
===

title: history_trung_nhi
---
Trưng Nhị: Do not mind the banner. It is heavier than it looks, and so am I.
-> You are the younger sister?
    Trưng Nhị: Trưng Nhị. Younger, and better with a bow. My sister speaks for us. I ride beside her. The stories say we rode elephants into battle. Have you tried to steer an elephant? We did.
-> Who fights for you?
    Trưng Nhị: Everyone. Many of our captains are women: Phùng Thị Chính, the stories say, fought with her baby on her back. Lê Chân raised an army on the coast. Here, a daughter can inherit, and a woman can lead. The Han find this very upsetting.
-> What is the drum for?
    Trưng Nhị: Listen to it and you will know. The bronze drums are older than us, older than the Han. When they sound, the Lạc lords come.
-> Goodbye.
    Trưng Nhị: Ride well. And if you meet an elephant, let it steer.
===

title: history_drum_caster
---
Drum Caster: Careful. It is still warm. Well, no. But it was, once, and I like to say that.
-> How do you make one?
    Drum Caster: Shape the drum in wax over a core of clay. Carve every picture into the wax. Cover it all in more clay, heat it, pour the wax out and the bronze in. Break the mould. If you did it right, the pictures come out in bronze. If not, you start again. I have started again a lot.
-> What are the pictures?
    Drum Caster: A star in the middle, for the sun. Around it, birds, and people in feathers dancing, and boats full of warriors. And on some, frogs on the rim. Frogs sing when the rain comes. A drum with frogs asks for rain, politely, and very loudly.
-> Who buys them?
    Drum Caster: Lords, for their feasts and their funerals. And far away: our drums go down the rivers and across the sea. People will dig them up in Thailand, in Malaysia, on islands deep in Indonesia. Loud diplomacy.
-> Why "Dong Son"?
    Drum Caster: That is your name for us, after the village by the river Mã where a fisherman found our things in 1924, and your scholars came to dig. We call it home.
-> Goodbye.
    Drum Caster: Hit it if you like. Not too hard. The frogs are sensitive.
===

title: history_harbour_master
---
Harbour Master of Palembang: Welcome to Srivijaya. Ship's name, cargo, port you sailed from. And the toll.
-> Why a toll?
    Harbour Master of Palembang: Every ship between India and China comes through our strait, or through the Sunda strait, which is also ours. We keep the pirates off the sea road, and the ships pay us for it. It is cheaper than being robbed. Usually.
-> What do you trade?
    Harbour Master of Palembang: Camphor and resins from our forests, gold from our rivers, cloves from the Spice Islands in the east, cloth from India, silk and jars from China. Everything stops here, everything is weighed here, and everything pays a little.
-> Is it true about the gold?
    Harbour Master of Palembang: The Arab sailors tell that every morning our king throws a brick of gold into the estuary. I tell them yes. Then I charge them more.
-> What happens next?
    Harbour Master of Palembang: In 1025 the Chola king Rajendra sends a fleet from south India and sacks our ports. We do not recover all of it. But about 1400 a prince of Palembang, Parameswara, will found Malacca on the same strait. Whoever holds it, the ships still pay.
-> Goodbye.
    Harbour Master of Palembang: Sail well. Pay on the way back too.
===

title: history_yijing
---
Yijing: Peace to you. I am Yijing, a monk from Tang China. I came here from Guangzhou on a Persian ship, in the year you call 671.
-> Why come here?
    Yijing: To go to India, to the great monastery of Nalanda, a monk must read Sanskrit. Srivijaya has more than a thousand monks, and they study as they do in India. I tell every monk from China: stop here first, a year or two. I wrote it down, so now it is advice.
-> Where next?
    Yijing: Nalanda. Ten years of study there. Then back here, to copy and translate. Once I went aboard a ship in this harbour to send a letter home for more ink and paper, and the ship sailed with me on it. I went to China by accident and came back.
-> What do you write?
    Yijing: How monks live in India and in the islands of the South Sea. And the lives of the monks who went west before me to look for the teachings. Many of them did not come home. Someone should remember them.
-> Goodbye.
    Yijing: Go in peace. If you sail, keep your ink dry.
===

title: history_relief_carver
---
Relief Carver of Borobudur: Mind the dust. Every panel on this hill goes under a chisel like mine. Two thousand six hundred and seventy-two of them, but who is counting? Me. I am counting.
-> What do they show?
    Relief Carver of Borobudur: Stories that teach, as you climb. And around the stories, life: houses, markets, musicians, farmers, and ships. I like carving the ships.
-> The ships?
    Relief Carver of Borobudur: Outriggers with sails of matting, like the ones in the harbours. Ships like these sail as far as Madagascar. One day someone will build one from my picture and sail it all the way to Africa. I would like to see that.
-> What is down at the foot?
    Relief Carver of Borobudur: The first panels, of people and what their deeds bring them. Before they are even finished, the builders will cover them with a great wall of stone, perhaps to hold the hill up. Your people find them again in 1885.
-> How long does it take?
    Relief Carver of Borobudur: Two generations, people say, maybe more. The stone comes out of the rivers, block by block. No mortar: each block holds the next. My grandfather started this gallery.
-> Goodbye.
    Relief Carver of Borobudur: Walk every gallery before you go up. That is the way. It is also good for the legs.
===

title: history_baray_engineer
---
Baray Engineer: Careful. That is not a lake. It is a reservoir. I made it. Well, many thousands of us made it. I made the plan.
-> What is it for?
    Baray Engineer: Water for the rice in the dry months, say some of your scholars. A sea around the mountain of the gods, say others. I say: both, and either way it took a great deal of digging. Eight kilometres of it.
-> Who does the digging?
    Baray Engineer: Every village owes work to the king and to the temples, so every village sends its people. The temple stones list them: dancers, cooks, potters, diggers, by the thousand.
-> And the stone?
    Baray Engineer: Sandstone from the Kulen hills, a long way off. It comes down the canals on rafts. Every block has holes for the pegs that lift it. Look for the holes: that is how you know someone worked very hard.
-> What happens to Angkor?
    Baray Engineer: Too much water, then too little. In the 1300s and 1400s great floods and long droughts break the canals. In 1431 the army of Ayutthaya takes the city, and the court moves south, near Phnom Penh. But Angkor Wat is never empty. Monks keep it to this day.
-> Goodbye.
    Baray Engineer: Do not swim in the moat. The crocodiles have not read the plan.
===

title: history_zhou_da_guide
---
Zhou Da-Guide: Welcome, welcome! Tour group, this way, keep up! I am Zhou Da-Guide, envoy of the Great Yuan, and the only visitor who wrote any of this down.
-> The only one?
    Zhou Da-Guide: The Khmer carved their kings and their temples on stone. Everything else they wrote on palm leaves and hides, and the damp ate it. So for the markets, the houses, the bathing and the lunch, there is only me. In 1296 I stayed a year. You are welcome.
-> What did you see?
    Zhou Da-Guide: The markets are run by women: any business, ask the wife. Everybody bathes in the river, many times a day, all together. The king rides out with a thousand followers and a golden sword. And there are slaves, taken from the hill peoples. I wrote that down too. A guide tells you everything.
-> Why the flag?
    Zhou Da-Guide: So you can find me in the crowd. One of the greatest cities in the world! Very crowded. Also I like flags.
-> Do you exaggerate?
    Zhou Da-Guide: A guide never exaggerates. A guide enhances. The towers were gold. Some of them. On top.
-> Goodbye.
    Zhou Da-Guide: The gift shop is on the left. It is a stall of lotus seeds. Highly recommended.
===

title: history_flame_potter
---
Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: Mind the pot. The clay is easy. The fire is the hard part.
-> Why the crests?
    Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: Your people call them flame pots. Flames, waves, I will not say. But look inside: soot on the outside, burnt soup at the bottom. We cook in them. A pot can be beautiful and still make dinner.
-> Do you farm?
    Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: No. We tend the chestnuts, gather nuts and shellfish, fish the bay, hunt deer and boar. And we stay: this town is lived in for fifteen hundred years. Your scholars once said people could not live in one place without farms. We did not hear them.
-> How old are pots like these?
    Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: Mine are new. But my people have made pots for ten thousand years already, since about 14,500 BCE. Among the oldest anywhere. People in what you call China made them earlier still; we do not mind sharing.
-> What happens to your people?
    Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: Rice farmers come from the continent long after me, and our children marry theirs. Everyone in Japan carries a little of us. The Ainu of the north and the Ryukyuans of the south carry the most.
-> Goodbye.
    Flame Potter of Sannai-Maruyama: Take the comb if you find it, the red one. That red is lacquer, from the urushi tree. We have used it for thousands of years.
===

title: history_chestnut_keeper
---
Chestnut Keeper: Chestnut? Roasted this morning. Mind, they are hot.
-> Did you plant them?
    Chestnut Keeper: We clear the other trees, and we keep the best chestnuts near the town. Now the woods around us are almost all chestnut. Is that farming? You decide. We were too busy eating.
-> What is the big structure?
    Chestnut Keeper: Six posts of chestnut, each a metre thick, sunk two metres into the ground. A tower to watch the bay? A stage for the dances? Your people built it again with three floors, but nobody knows what it held. Climb it and have a guess.
-> How many live here?
    Chestnut Keeper: Hundreds, in the busiest times, your diggers say. Pit houses, long houses, store houses, a road, a burial ground. A town, without a single field.
-> Goodbye.
    Chestnut Keeper: Watch out for the bear. She likes the chestnuts too.
===

title: history_jade_carver
---
Jade Carver of Fengtian: Hold it up to the light. Green as a river.
-> How do you cut jade?
    Jade Carver of Fengtian: You do not cut it. You wear it away: a cord, wet sand, back and forth, for days. It is harder than your knife. Patience is harder than jade.
-> Where does it go?
    Jade Carver of Fengtian: Later, Fengtian jade from here goes by boat to the Philippines, to Vietnam, to Thailand, to Borneo: rings with a slit, earrings with three points. One of the longest sea roads of any stone in the ancient world.
-> Who are your people?
    Jade Carver of Fengtian: Our language is the grandmother of languages from Madagascar to Hawaii. Your scholars sorted them into branches: of the ten oldest, nine grow only here. The grandchildren leave in canoes. The grandmothers stay.
-> Goodbye.
    Jade Carver of Fengtian: Mind the sand. It gets everywhere. That is how jade is made.
===

title: history_nankuanli_farmer
---
Farmer of Nankuanli: Mind the dog. She minds you.
-> What do you grow?
    Farmer of Nankuanli: Millet on the slope, rice in the wet ground. Both came over the strait from the mainland with our ancestors, long ago. We brought the dogs too.
-> The dog?
    Farmer of Nankuanli: At Nankuanli we bury our dogs with care, as we bury people. Your diggers will find them, four thousand eight hundred years from now. She is a good dog. She will be a famous one.
-> Do you sail?
    Farmer of Nankuanli: Some of us. My cousin's children went south over the channel to the islands you call the Batanes and Luzon. Every generation, a few more islands. Their grandchildren will reach places we have no names for.
-> Goodbye.
    Farmer of Nankuanli: Take some millet for the boat. The dog will see you to the edge of the field.
===

title: history_queen_seondeok
---
Queen Seondeok: You stand in Seorabeol, the capital of Silla. I am Seondeok. Yes, the queen.
-> The first queen?
    Queen Seondeok: The first to rule Silla in her own name, from 632 to 647. Two more queens will follow me. The story goes that the Tang emperor sent me a painting of peonies with no bees and no butterflies, to say that a queen without a husband is a flower without scent. I said: then these peonies have no scent. I planted the seeds. I was right.
-> The tower?
    Queen Seondeok: Cheomseongdae. Built in my reign to watch the sky. The calendar, the seasons, the eclipses: a kingdom must know when to plant, and when to worry.
-> Your crown?
    Queen Seondeok: Gold, cut like trees and antlers, hung with jade. Silla is the golden kingdom. The story goes that Arab geographers will write that gold is so common here even the dogs wear it. They exaggerate. A little.
-> What comes next?
    Queen Seondeok: Within thirty years Silla unites most of the peninsula, with Tang help and then against it, by 676. Silla lasts until 935. Not bad for a kingdom some said should not have a queen.
-> Goodbye.
    Queen Seondeok: Go well. Smell the peonies on your way out. Or do not. You know the story now.
===

title: history_star_watcher
---
Star Watcher of Cheomseongdae: Quiet, please. The stars are speaking. Slowly.
-> What do you watch?
    Star Watcher of Cheomseongdae: The sun's shadow for the seasons, the moon for the calendar, and comets and eclipses for the queen's worries. I write it all down. The court reads it when something goes wrong.
-> How old is this tower?
    Star Watcher of Cheomseongdae: New, in my time. Still standing in yours: the oldest standing observatory in East Asia. People count three hundred and sixty-two stones and twenty-seven courses, and argue about what the numbers mean. I just climb it.
-> How do you get in?
    Star Watcher of Cheomseongdae: A ladder to the window halfway up, then inside, then another ladder to the top. Very cosy. Bring a coat.
-> Goodbye.
    Star Watcher of Cheomseongdae: Clear skies to you.
===

title: history_li_qingzhao
---
Li Qingzhao: Searching, seeking. Cold, clear. Forgive me, I am working on a line.
-> Who are you?
    Li Qingzhao: A poet. I write ci: songs to old tunes, with new words. The scholars of my day said a woman's poems were a pastime. They read mine anyway, and so do you, nine hundred years later.
-> Why did you come south?
    Li Qingzhao: In 1127 the Jurchen took Kaifeng, the capital, and two emperors with it. The court fled south and made Hangzhou its home. My husband and I fled with fifteen carts of books, bronzes and rubbings of old stones. Most were lost on the road. He died two years later.
-> What did you collect?
    Li Qingzhao: Inscriptions on bronze and stone, the oldest writing we could find. After dinner we made tea and tested each other: which book, which chapter, which page holds this line? The winner drank first. Once I laughed so hard I spilled the tea down my front.
-> Goodbye.
    Li Qingzhao: Go slowly round the lake. It is better than any poem about it.
===

title: history_money_printer
---
Money Printer of Hangzhou: Fresh money! Do not touch it, the ink is wet.
-> Paper is money?
    Money Printer of Hangzhou: Copper coins are heavy. Strings of a thousand, carts of them. In Sichuan the merchants began to write notes for their iron coins; in 1023 the state took the printing over. Here in Hangzhou we have printed the huizi since 1160. Paper money printed by a government: we were the first.
-> How do you stop forgers?
    Money Printer of Hangzhou: A bronze plate, cut with care. Paper made from mulberry bark. A border of pictures, the text, and red seals in more than one colour. And a sentence printed on the note itself: forgers will be beheaded. That helps.
-> Any problems?
    Money Printer of Hangzhou: Wars cost money. When the court needs money, it asks me for more notes. More notes, and each one buys less. By the end of the dynasty we have learned that lesson more than once. So will you.
-> Goodbye.
    Money Printer of Hangzhou: Spend it soon. It is worth more today than tomorrow.
===

title: history_murasaki_shikibu
---
Murasaki Shikibu: I see more than I say. It is better for a writer.
-> What are you writing?
    Murasaki Shikibu: A long tale of Prince Genji, the Shining One, and the women in his life. Fifty-four chapters, some four hundred people. The court ladies pass the chapters round as I finish them. Some people call it the first novel. I call it unfinished.
-> Why do you write in kana?
    Murasaki Shikibu: Men write the official things in Chinese. Kana are our own letters for our own sounds, and in kana a woman may write whatever she sees. I can read Chinese too. When my brother studied the classics I learned them faster, just by listening. My father sighed: if only she had been born a boy.
-> Is Murasaki your name?
    Murasaki Shikibu: It is a nickname, perhaps after a lady in my book; Shikibu is from my father's office. My real name? Nobody wrote it down. You will find that a great deal of history is like that.
-> Do you know Sei Shōnagon?
    Murasaki Shikibu: I know her book. She served the other empress, Teishi; I serve Empress Shōshi. In my diary I wrote that she is dreadfully conceited, and scatters her Chinese about. I stand by it. Read her anyway. She is good.
-> Goodbye.
    Murasaki Shikibu: Mind the pond. The carp are watching.
===

title: history_sei_shonagon
---
Sei Shōnagon: In spring, the dawn. In summer, the night. Do you agree? You should.
-> What is The Pillow Book?
    Sei Shōnagon: Notes, kept by my pillow. Lists of things: splendid things, hateful things, things that make the heart beat faster. Little stories of the court. I wrote what I liked. People still read it a thousand years later, so I was right to.
-> What are hateful things?
    Sei Shōnagon: A visitor who talks on and on when you are in a hurry. A mosquito's thin little voice when you are falling asleep. A carriage with squeaky wheels. That is going on the list: you, standing there.
-> And splendid things?
    Sei Shōnagon: Snow on the roof. A letter that comes just when you wanted it. A clever answer at exactly the right moment. I am known for those.
-> What about Murasaki?
    Sei Shōnagon: She called me dreadfully conceited, in her diary. I never mentioned her in my book. That is how you win.
-> Goodbye.
    Sei Shōnagon: Go well. Do not be hateful on the way.
===

title: history_king_sejong
---
King Sejong: Welcome to Hanyang. Can you read? Good. Soon everyone here will.
-> What are the letters?
    King Sejong: Our speech is not Chinese, so Chinese letters do not fit it, and only a few scholars can spend the years to learn them. Many of my people have something to say and cannot write it. I have made twenty-eight letters, easy to learn, for every day. This year, 1446, I give them to the people: the Hunminjeongeum, the correct sounds to teach the people.
-> How do they work?
    King Sejong: Each consonant is a picture of the mouth that says it: the back of the tongue against the throat, the tip of the tongue, the lips, the teeth, the throat itself. The vowels are made of heaven, earth and humankind: a dot, a flat line, a standing line. The letters of a syllable sit together in a block.
-> Did everyone like them?
    King Sejong: No. Some scholars wrote to me that only barbarians make their own letters. I disagreed. Today Korea keeps a day for the letters: 9 October in the South, 15 January in the North. Call them Hangul or Chosŏn'gŭl; they are the same letters.
-> What else have you done?
    King Sejong: My court has charted the skies, measured the rain in every province, made clocks that ring themselves, and printed books with metal type. I gather clever people and let them work. Jang Yeong-sil, by the water clock, is one of the best.
-> Goodbye.
    King Sejong: Learn them by lunch. The wise ones do.
===

title: history_jang_yeongsil
---
Jang Yeong-sil: Measure twice. Then measure again.
-> Who are you?
    Jang Yeong-sil: An engineer of the king. I was born a government slave; my mother was of that rank. King Taejong noticed my skill and King Sejong made me an official. Few men of my birth have ever stood in this palace.
-> What is the water clock?
    Jang Yeong-sil: Water flows from the high vessels into the tall one at a steady rate. A float rises, and as it rises it drops bronze balls that set wooden figures moving. They strike a bell for the hours, and a drum and a gong for the watches of the night. Nobody has to stay awake to strike them.
-> And the rain gauge?
    Jang Yeong-sil: A bronze cylinder on a stone stand, the same size in every province, and a ruler to read the rain. People say I made it. The court record says the crown prince did, in 1441. Either way, every province now measures rain the same way, and sends the numbers to the capital. Some two centuries before Europe does the same.
-> What happens to you?
    Jang Yeong-sil: In 1442 a royal sedan chair I built breaks on the road. I am dismissed. After that the records say nothing more about me. Remember the clocks, if you would.
-> Goodbye.
    Jang Yeong-sil: Mind the bear on the terrace. It does not care how clever you are.
===

title: history_muslin_weaver
---
Muslin Weaver of Dhaka: Woven air, they call it. Hold it to the light: you will see your hand through it.
-> How fine is it?
    Muslin Weaver of Dhaka: The finest pieces took months on the loom. A length long enough to wrap a person could pass through a finger ring. Poets gave the best cloths names: running water, evening dew.
-> Who buys it?
    Muslin Weaver of Dhaka: The Mughal court first: Bengal is the richest province of the empire. Then merchants from Persia, Armenia, the Netherlands, France and England. The Company's ships carry it to Europe. In Paris, later, the fine ladies will wear nothing else.
-> What happens to the trade?
    Muslin Weaver of Dhaka: After 1757 the Company rules Bengal, and its agents set the price of our work. Then the mills of Lancashire make cheap cloth by machine, and send it here, with no tax to stop it. By the 1800s the great looms of Dhaka are quiet. Go down to the sea, to the ships that carried it. Go east, to Calcutta, to see what came next.
-> Goodbye.
    Muslin Weaver of Dhaka: Mind the gharial. He does not care for cloth.
===

title: history_cotton_spinner
---
Spinner of Dhaka: Quiet, please. The thread is thinner than a hair.
-> Where does the cotton come from?
    Spinner of Dhaka: From phuti karpas, a cotton that grows along the banks of the Meghna. Short fibres, very soft. No other cotton spins so fine. Later it is lost, and the finest muslin with it; in your time, people are trying to grow it again.
-> How do you spin it?
    Spinner of Dhaka: Early in the morning, by the river, while the air is damp: dry air snaps the thread. Before that, we clean the cotton with the jawbone of the boal fish. Its teeth are as fine as a comb. Then the spindle, and wet fingers, and patience.
-> Who spins?
    Spinner of Dhaka: Women, mostly. Young eyes and light fingers spin the finest. The weavers get the fame. Now you know better.
-> Goodbye.
    Spinner of Dhaka: Go gently. You are walking through my morning.
===

title: history_ram_mohan_roy
---
Raja Ram Mohan Roy: Read, and then argue. Here: today's paper.
-> Who are you?
    Raja Ram Mohan Roy: A writer of Calcutta. I publish newspapers in Bengali and Persian, I translate, I argue. I have argued for schools that teach science, for the right of women to inherit property, and for a free press.
-> What did you argue against?
    Raja Ram Mohan Roy: Sati: the burning of widows on their husbands' funeral fires. I saw it done to my own brother's wife. I wrote against it for years. In 1829 the government of Bengal banned it.
-> What do you think of the Company?
    Raja Ram Mohan Roy: It has brought printing presses and new ideas; it has also taken the land's wealth and given us little say. I want both things said, and I want Indians to say them, in Indian newspapers.
-> Goodbye.
    Raja Ram Mohan Roy: I go to England next, to speak for the emperor in Delhi. Wish me a calm sea.
===

title: history_rani_lakshmibai
---
Rani Lakshmibai: Stand straight. You are in the presence of the Rani of Jhansi.
-> What happened to Jhansi?
    Rani Lakshmibai: When my husband the Raja died, the Company would not accept our adopted son as his heir. By its "doctrine of lapse" a kingdom with no son of the blood falls to the Company. So it took Jhansi. I said: I will not give up my Jhansi.
-> What was 1857?
    Rani Lakshmibai: The Company's own soldiers rose first, at Meerut, and then princes, landlords and peasants across the north. The British called it a mutiny. Many in India call it the First War of Independence. It was fought hard on both sides, and cruelly.
-> What happens to you?
    Rani Lakshmibai: In 1858 I fight at Jhansi, and then at Gwalior, and there I die, in a cavalry uniform, with a sword. The same year the British Crown takes India from the Company. The Company is ended, and so is the old Mughal court.
-> Goodbye.
    Rani Lakshmibai: Ride well, or do not ride.
===

title: history_rabindranath_tagore
---
Rabindranath Tagore: Ah, a traveller. Sit, if you like. I am writing a song.
-> Who are you?
    Rabindranath Tagore: A poet, mostly. Also a composer, a painter in my old age, a teacher at my school in Santiniketan, and a writer of stories, plays and letters. Too many letters.
-> The Nobel Prize?
    Rabindranath Tagore: In 1913, for Gitanjali, "song offerings". The first Nobel to anyone outside Europe. It surprised the newspapers more than it surprised me.
-> Do people still sing your songs?
    Rabindranath Tagore: I wrote more than two thousand. Two countries now sing mine as their national anthems: Bangladesh sings "Amar Shonar Bangla", "My golden Bengal", and India sings "Jana Gana Mana". Not bad for one old man.
-> Did you ever say no to an honour?
    Rabindranath Tagore: In 1919 British troops fired on a crowd at Amritsar and killed hundreds. I gave my knighthood back. Some honours are heavier than others.
-> Goodbye.
    Rabindranath Tagore: Where the mind is without fear, and the head is held high: go there.
===

title: history_begum_rokeya
---
Begum Rokeya: Educate the girls. Then see what happens.
-> Who are you?
    Begum Rokeya: A writer and a teacher. Girls in my family were not supposed to learn Bengali or English, so I learned them at night, by candlelight, with my brother's help. My husband encouraged me to write.
-> What is Sultana's Dream?
    Begum Rokeya: A story I wrote in 1905, in English, to see if I could. A woman dreams of Ladyland, where women run the country with science. They cook with sunlight, pull water from the clouds, and fly air-cars. The men stay at home, in seclusion, where they cannot cause trouble.
-> Does it have a war?
    Begum Rokeya: An army invades, and the men are no use. The women of the university turn the sun's heat on the soldiers, and the soldiers run away. Nobody is hurt. Science, you see.
-> What did you do after?
    Begum Rokeya: In 1911 I opened a school for Muslim girls in Calcutta, with eight pupils. It is still open. In Bangladesh they keep 9 December, my birthday, as Rokeya Day.
-> Goodbye.
    Begum Rokeya: Take the air-car. It is quicker than walking, and nobody has crashed one yet.
===

title: history_language_student
---
Student of Dhaka, 1952: We want Bengali as a state language! You can shout it with me.
-> What is the matter?
    Student of Dhaka, 1952: Pakistan has two halves, a thousand miles apart. Most of its people live here in the east and speak Bengali. In 1948 its leaders said Urdu, and only Urdu, would be the state language. We said no.
-> What happened on 21 February?
    Student of Dhaka, 1952: Gatherings were banned. We marched from the university anyway. The police fired. Salam, Barkat, Rafiq, Jabbar and others were killed. The day is called Ekushey: "twenty-one".
-> Did it work?
    Student of Dhaka, 1952: In 1956 Bengali became a state language of Pakistan. And since 2000 the whole world keeps 21 February as International Mother Language Day, for every language, large and small.
-> Goodbye.
    Student of Dhaka, 1952: Speak your language to your children. That is all we asked.
===

title: history_freedom_fighter_poet
---
Poet of 1971: Shh, listen. The radio. They are still broadcasting.
-> What happened in 1971?
    Poet of 1971: In December 1970 our party won the election across Pakistan, and the government would not hand over power. On 25 March 1971 the army struck Dhaka. Nine months of war followed.
-> How bad was it?
    Poet of 1971: Hundreds of thousands were killed, perhaps far more; many call it a genocide. Some ten million people fled to India. In the last days, many of our teachers, doctors and writers were taken and killed.
-> How did it end?
    Poet of 1971: Our fighters, the Mukti Bahini, fought the whole nine months, and in December India joined the war. On 16 December 1971 the army in Dhaka surrendered. Bangladesh was free.
-> Goodbye.
    Poet of 1971: Every 21 February we walk barefoot to the Shaheed Minar with flowers, before dawn. Come with us one year.
===

title: history_marajoara_potter
---
Marajoara Potter: Careful, the paint is wet. Red, black, white.
-> Where are we?
    Marajoara Potter: Marajó, an island as big as a country, where the great river meets the sea. Half the year the land floods, so we build our houses on mounds of earth, high and dry. Some mounds hold a whole village.
-> Tell me about the pots.
    Marajoara Potter: Bowls, jars, figures: painted in bands and mazes, red, black and white, some carved as well. They last. A thousand years later your museums will keep them behind glass, and people will lean in close to look, just as you are doing now.
-> Was this a big place?
    Marajoara Potter: For a thousand years, from about 400 to 1400 in your counting. Many mounds, many villages, roads of canoes. People who come later will call the Amazon empty. It was never empty.
-> Goodbye.
    Marajoara Potter: Mind the caiman in the flooded forest. Ride a lily leaf; they are stronger than they look.
===

title: history_dark_earth_gardener
---
Gardener of the Dark Earth: Charcoal, pottery, fish bones. The recipe for soil.
-> What is dark earth?
    Gardener of the Dark Earth: The forest soil is thin and poor: the rain washes it out. So we burn slowly, not to ash but to charcoal, and add it to the earth with our waste, our bones, our broken pots. The earth turns black and stays rich. Your people call it terra preta.
-> Does it last?
    Gardener of the Dark Earth: Two thousand years and more. Farmers dig it today and sell it by the truckload. Your scientists study it to learn how to keep carbon in the ground. We just wanted good manioc.
-> Is the forest wild?
    Gardener of the Dark Earth: Brazil nuts, cacao, palms, fruit trees: near our towns the forest is full of the trees we liked and planted. Under it, in your time, lidar finds towns, causeways and reservoirs. The first Spaniards down the river, in 1542, saw towns along the banks. The Europeans who came later saw a wilderness: the sicknesses that came with them had emptied it first.
-> Goodbye.
    Gardener of the Dark Earth: Plant something before you go. It is polite.
===

title: history_zumbi
---
Zumbi dos Palmares: You found Palmares. Few of our enemies do. Are you an enemy?
-> What is Palmares?
    Zumbi dos Palmares: A free country in the hills, for people who escaped the sugar plantations. It began about 1605. Now there are thousands of us, in many villages, with fields, forges, and our own laws. Africans, mostly, from Kongo and Ndongo and elsewhere; also Indigenous people, and some poor Portuguese who had nowhere else.
-> Who leads it?
    Zumbi dos Palmares: Ganga Zumba led us before me. In 1678 he made a peace with the governor: freedom for those born in Palmares, if they moved, and the others sent back. I refused it. Freedom for some is not freedom. Since then, I lead.
-> What happens?
    Zumbi dos Palmares: For nearly a century Palmares beats off expedition after expedition. In 1694 a great army with cannon takes our capital. On 20 November 1695 I am betrayed and killed. Brazil now keeps that day as Black Consciousness Day, a holiday for the whole country.
-> Goodbye.
    Zumbi dos Palmares: Go well. Tell them Palmares was free.
===

title: history_dandara
---
Dandara: Plant, then defend what you planted.
-> Who are you?
    Dandara: Dandara, of Palmares. The people who wrote things down wrote little about me. Palmares remembers me in its stories: I farmed, I hunted, I fought, and I stood with Zumbi.
-> Is it true you knew capoeira?
    Dandara: So the stories say. Capoeira grew among the enslaved in Brazil, fighting disguised as a dance; some say it began in places like this. Nobody wrote down the first lesson. Want the second?
-> What happened to you?
    Dandara: The story goes that in 1694, when Palmares fell, I chose death over slavery. Remember the story, and ask who kept it: not the governors. The people did.
-> Goodbye.
    Dandara: Mind the jaguar by the palisade. She is not on our side, or theirs.
===

title: history_palmares_smith
---
Smith of Palmares: Hot iron waits for nobody. Stand back.
-> Where did you learn to forge?
    Smith of Palmares: Many of us came from the lands of Kongo and Ndongo, where a smith is a respected person and iron is old knowledge. Here we work scrap from the plantations and ore from the hills.
-> What do you make?
    Smith of Palmares: Hoes, axes, knives, nails, and when we must, weapons. Palmares grows maize, beans, manioc, bananas and sugar cane, and feeds thousands. Fields need tools, and tools need me.
-> Do you trade?
    Smith of Palmares: With the small farms below, when they will trade. Some sell us salt and cloth and say nothing to the governor. Freedom needs friends as well as tools.
-> Goodbye.
    Smith of Palmares: Take the hoe in the chest on the hill. I made it this morning.
===

title: history_andre_reboucas
---
André Rebouças: Mind the tram. Welcome to Rio, in a month of flowers.
-> Who are you?
    André Rebouças: An engineer. I built docks for this city and railways inland, and I teach at the Polytechnic School. My grandmother was enslaved; my father became a lawyer and a deputy. I am one of the few Black men at the emperor's court, and I use my place there.
-> What happened on 13 May?
    André Rebouças: The Golden Law. Two lines long: slavery is ended in Brazil, from this day; all laws against this are revoked. The crowds threw flowers in the street for days. We were the last country in the Americas to do it.
-> Is it enough?
    André Rebouças: No. Freedom without land, schools or work is half a freedom. I argue for land for the freed: a rural democracy. The landowners who lost their slaves are angry, and the next year they help to end the empire.
-> What happens to you?
    André Rebouças: In 1889 the Republic comes, and I go into exile with the emperor's family. I die in Madeira in 1898. I never come home. Remember the docks; they are still there.
-> Goodbye.
    André Rebouças: Measure twice, build once.
===

title: history_luis_gama
---
Luís Gama: Next case. Oh, you are not a client. Sit anyway.
-> Who are you?
    Luís Gama: A lawyer, a poet and a journalist of São Paulo. I was born free in Bahia. When I was ten my father, a Portuguese nobleman, sold me into slavery to pay his debts.
-> How did you become free?
    Luís Gama: At seventeen I learned to read, and proved that I had been born free. I ran, I became a soldier, then a clerk, then a lawyer without a degree. They let me practise; they regretted it.
-> What did you do in court?
    Luís Gama: Brazil banned the African slave trade in 1831. Every African brought in after that was held against the law, and I said so, in court, case after case. I won freedom for hundreds of people.
-> Goodbye.
    Luís Gama: I die in 1882, six years before the Golden Law. Thousands walk behind my coffin. Do not let them say the law freed everyone by itself.
===

title: history_dragao_do_mar
---
Dragão do Mar: The sea is free. So is everyone on it.
-> Who are you?
    Dragão do Mar: Francisco José do Nascimento, jangadeiro of Fortaleza, in Ceará. A jangada is a raft of logs and a tall sail; we fish from them, and we carry things out to the ships, because the port has no pier.
-> What did you do?
    Dragão do Mar: In 1881 the slave dealers wanted our jangadas to carry the enslaved out to the ships, to be sold to the plantations of the south. We refused, every one of us. We said: in the port of Ceará no more slaves are shipped. And none were.
-> And then?
    Dragão do Mar: In 1884 Ceará freed all its enslaved people, four years before the rest of Brazil. My jangada was carried to Rio and through its streets, and they called me the Dragon of the Sea. Ride her over the bay; she likes a passenger.
-> Goodbye.
    Dragão do Mar: Fair winds. Do not let anyone tell you that one refusal is too small.
===

title: history_hojo_tokimune
---
Hōjō Tokimune: You came by sea? Then you have seen the bay. Did you see any ships with Mongol flags?
-> Who are you?
    Hōjō Tokimune: The regent. The shogun is a child, and the emperor lives in Kyoto and writes poems. My family, the Hōjō, governs Japan from Kamakura. Since 1268, that means me.
-> What did Kublai Khan want?
    Hōjō Tokimune: Everything. His letter called our emperor 'the king of a little country' and asked us to be friends, or else. Korea had already given in. The court in Kyoto wanted to send a polite answer. I sent no answer.
-> What happened then?
    Hōjō Tokimune: In 1274 they landed here at Hakata with Korean ships and soldiers, with bombs and poisoned arrows, and fought in groups, not one champion against another. Then a storm, and they sailed home. So we built the wall. In 1281 they came with some four thousand ships. They never got past the wall, and in August a typhoon sank most of them.
-> Goodbye.
    Hōjō Tokimune: Watch the sea.
===
title: history_takezaki_suenaga
---
Takezaki Suenaga: Takezaki Suenaga, of Higo! You have heard of me? No? You will. I have a scroll.
-> What did you do?
    Takezaki Suenaga: In 1274 I went first into the fight, with my five men. My horse was shot under me; I would have died if Shiraishi had not come. In 1281 I rowed out at night and boarded a Mongol ship. Look at the scroll: it is all there.
-> Why a scroll?
    Takezaki Suenaga: After the first war I got nothing. Nobody in Kamakura knew my name. So I rode all the way there and told the magistrate my story myself. He gave me a horse and a piece of land. Then I paid painters, so that nobody would ever doubt it.
-> Was it vanity?
    Takezaki Suenaga: Probably. But without my vanity you would know almost nothing of what the Mongol soldiers looked like, how they fought, or what their bombs were. Your museum in Tokyo is glad I was vain.
-> Goodbye.
    Takezaki Suenaga: Tell them you met me!
===
title: history_matsuo_basho
---
Matsuo Bashō: The road out of Edo starts here. Are you going far?
-> Who are you?
    Matsuo Bashō: A poet who keeps leaving home. I write haikai: short poems of five, seven and five sounds, about small things that are not small.
-> Where did you go?
    Matsuo Bashō: In 1689 my friend Sora and I walked north, to the far end of Honshu and back by the Sea of Japan: some 2,400 kilometres in five months. I wrote The Narrow Road to the Deep North about it. 'The months and days are travellers of a hundred ages.'
-> Tell me the frog poem.
    Matsuo Bashō: An old pond. A frog jumps in. The sound of water. ... Yes, that is all of it. People have written whole books about it. I wrote it about a frog.
-> Goodbye.
    Matsuo Bashō: Go well. Look at things.
===
title: history_hokusai
---
Katsushika Hokusai: Don't jog my arm. ... All right, what?
-> Who are you?
    Katsushika Hokusai: Today? Gakyō Rōjin, the Old Man Mad About Painting. I have had more than thirty names. People called me Hokusai, so call me that.
-> Is that the great wave?
    Katsushika Hokusai: Under the Wave off Kanagawa, one of my Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji. Look: Fuji is the small one, at the bottom. The printers made thousands. They sold for the price of a double bowl of noodles. Later the whole world copied it. Nobody paid me for that.
-> How old are you?
    Katsushika Hokusai: Over seventy. Nothing I drew before seventy is worth much. At eighty I will be better, at ninety I will see the secret of things, and at a hundred and ten every dot will be alive. That is the plan.
-> Goodbye.
    Katsushika Hokusai: Shut the door. No, leave it. We are moving again next week.
===
title: history_katsushika_oi
---
Katsushika Ōi: If you want my father, he is drawing. If you want a painting finished, you want me.
-> Who are you?
    Katsushika Ōi: Ōi, Hokusai's third daughter. I married a painter once; I laughed at his pictures and he sent me home. Since then I paint with my father. We live in the mess and move when it gets too bad.
-> Did you paint his pictures?
    Katsushika Ōi: Some of them. Some books with his name have my hands in them. He says that in pictures of women, I am better than he is. He does not say that very often, so I say it for him.
-> What do you paint?
    Katsushika Ōi: Light. A lantern at night, the faces by it, the dark behind. My father paints the wave and the mountain. I paint the room where someone is reading late.
-> Goodbye.
    Katsushika Ōi: Mind the ink.
===
title: history_edo_tea_keeper
---
Tea Keeper of the Tōkaidō: Sit, sit! Tea, and a rice cake? The road is long.
-> Where are we?
    Tea Keeper of the Tōkaidō: At the edge of Edo, on the Tōkaidō: the road to Kyoto, with fifty-three post stations. It starts at Nihonbashi, the bridge just over there. Every distance in Japan is measured from it.
-> Who uses the road?
    Tea Keeper of the Tōkaidō: Everyone. The lords must live in Edo every other year, so they march up and down with hundreds of men. Then the pilgrims, the merchants, the poets, the actors. Good for tea.
-> And the ninja?
    Tea Keeper of the Tōkaidō: In the theatre, a man in black is the stagehand: we all agree not to see him. Then the playwrights dressed their assassins in black too, so the audience got a fright. Real spies? They dressed like me.
-> Goodbye.
    Tea Keeper of the Tōkaidō: Safe journey. Fifty-two more stations!
===
title: history_edo_fishmonger
---
Fishmonger of Nihonbashi: Bonito! First bonito of the year! You look like a person who wants a bonito.
-> Where are we?
    Fishmonger of Nihonbashi: Nihonbashi, the heart of Edo. A million people live in this city, more than in almost any city in the world, and they all eat fish. The market starts before dawn and is done by noon.
-> Why is the first bonito special?
    Fishmonger of Nihonbashi: It is the first of the season, so everyone must have it before the neighbours do. People pawn their clothes for it. In Edo we say: never let money spend the night with you.
-> What about the shogun?
    Fishmonger of Nihonbashi: The shogun's family has ruled for two hundred years. No war, few foreign ships, a great many rules. The merchants are supposed to be at the bottom. Look at this street, and tell me who is at the bottom.
-> Goodbye.
    Fishmonger of Nihonbashi: Come back tomorrow. Earlier!
===
title: history_fukuzawa_yukichi
---
Fukuzawa Yukichi: Ah, a visitor. Do you read English? Dutch? No matter. Read something.
-> Who are you?
    Fukuzawa Yukichi: A teacher. I learned Dutch, then found that the world spoke English, so I learned that. I went to America in 1860 and to Europe in 1862, and came home and wrote down everything I saw. My school is Keiō.
-> What did you write?
    Fukuzawa Yukichi: 'Heaven does not make one person above another, nor below.' That was 1872, in An Encouragement of Learning. Study, and you can be anything; do not study, and you will be someone else's servant. It sold in the millions.
-> Is that all?
    Fukuzawa Yukichi: No. In 1885 an essay, perhaps mine, said Japan should leave Asia behind and act as the Western powers did. Japan did. Remember that too, when you see my face on old banknotes.
-> Goodbye.
    Fukuzawa Yukichi: Learn something today.
===
title: history_tsuda_umeko
---
Tsuda Umeko: Good afternoon. Do you prefer English or Japanese? I am better at one of them.
-> Who are you?
    Tsuda Umeko: In 1871 the government sent five girls to America with the Iwakura Mission, to learn and come back. I was six, the youngest. I lived in Washington for eleven years.
-> What happened when you came home?
    Tsuda Umeko: I had forgotten my Japanese. I could not read it. And there was no work in Japan for an educated woman, only marriage. I said no to the marriage.
-> So what did you do?
    Tsuda Umeko: I studied biology in America, then came home and taught. In 1900 I founded a school of English for women in Tokyo. It is Tsuda University now. Since 2024 my face is on the 5,000 yen note. I would rather you read my students.
-> Goodbye.
    Tsuda Umeko: Goodbye. Study hard.
===
title: history_kusumoto_ine
---
Kusumoto Ine: Are you hurt? No? Then wash your hands anyway.
-> Who are you?
    Kusumoto Ine: A doctor. My mother was a woman of Nagasaki, Taki. My father was Philipp Franz von Siebold, a German doctor at the Dutch trading post. When I was two he was thrown out of Japan for having maps he should not have had.
-> How did you learn medicine?
    Kusumoto Ine: From his students, one town after another: surgery, then obstetrics. People said a woman could not, and a child of a foreigner could not. I did both.
-> What did you do?
    Kusumoto Ine: I delivered babies, in Nagasaki and then in Tokyo. In 1873 I was called to the imperial court to deliver a child there. Japan's first woman doctor of Western medicine, they say. I was too busy to notice.
-> Goodbye.
    Kusumoto Ine: Wash your hands!
===

title: history_katanda_fisher
---
Katanda Fisher: Quiet. The fish can hear you. The hippos too, and they are less polite.
-> Where are we?
    Katanda Fisher: On the Semliki, the river that runs out of the big lake toward the north. Fish, shellfish, antelope at the water. A good place. People have fished here for a very long time.
-> What is that?
    Katanda Fisher: A harpoon. Bone, ground smooth, with barbs cut along one side so the fish stays on. Down the river at Katanda your scientists found points like this. They say they may be 90,000 years old, older than almost anything like them anywhere.
-> Why does it matter?
    Katanda Fisher: People once said that clever tools began in Europe, late. Then they dug here. Clever began in Africa, early, and it took the fish with it.
-> Goodbye.
    Katanda Fisher: Mind the hippos. I mean it.
===
title: history_ishango_carver
---
Ishango Carver: Eleven. Thirteen. Seventeen. Nineteen. ... Sorry. Counting.
-> What are you making?
    Ishango Carver: A handle, from the leg bone of a baboon, with a chip of quartz in the end to cut with. And notches. Many notches, in groups, in three columns.
-> What do the notches mean?
    Ishango Carver: Your scholars cannot agree. One column holds 11, 13, 17 and 19: the primes between ten and twenty. Another holds 9, 19, 21 and 11. Some say it is arithmetic. Some say it counts the days of the moon. Some say the notches only help a hand to hold it. I am not telling.
-> Where is it now?
    Ishango Carver: In Brussels, in a museum. A Belgian scientist dug it up in 1950, when Belgium ruled the Congo. Many things went from here to Belgium. Ask about the others.
-> Goodbye.
    Ishango Carver: Twenty-one. Twenty-three... Goodbye.
===
title: history_raffia_weaver
---
Raffia Weaver: Feel this. No, gently. Softer than you thought?
-> What is it?
    Raffia Weaver: Cloth of raffia palm fibre, cut and beaten and woven, then cut again into a pile like velvet. The Portuguese called it velvet. Their kings and princes kept our cloth in their treasure rooms.
-> Is it money?
    Raffia Weaver: Some of it. A cloth of a certain size is worth a certain amount: you can pay taxes in it, or a bride-price. The other money is nzimbu, little shells from the island off Luanda. Only the king's people may gather them.
-> And the Portuguese?
    Raffia Weaver: They came in 1483. Our king took their faith, their letters and their teachers. Then their ships began to want people, not cloth. Ask the king. He is writing about it now.
-> Goodbye.
    Raffia Weaver: Go well. Keep your hands off the loom.
===
title: history_afonso_i
---
Afonso I: Sit. I am writing to my brother the king of Portugal. Again.
-> Who are you?
    Afonso I: Mvemba a Nzinga, Manikongo, king of Kongo since 1509. In baptism, Afonso. I sent my sons to study in Lisbon, built schools, and wrote to the kings of Portugal as one king to another.
-> What is the letter about?
    Afonso I: The trade. 'Each day the traders are taking our people, sons of this land and sons of our nobles and vassals, and even of our own family.' I asked Portugal to send teachers and doctors and medicine, and to stop the ships of slaves.
-> Did they stop?
    Afonso I: No. Their island of São Tomé needed people for its sugar, and the traders had friends in Lisbon. I made rules: every sale must be checked by my officials. The trade grew anyway. It grew for three hundred years.
-> Goodbye.
    Afonso I: Go in peace. Remember that we said no.
===
title: history_ne_vunda
---
Antonio Manuel ne Vunda: I am expected in Rome. Is this the road to Rome?
-> Who are you?
    Antonio Manuel ne Vunda: Antonio Manuel ne Vunda, of the house of the king of Kongo. In 1604 King Álvaro II sent me to Rome as his ambassador, to speak for Kongo without Portugal speaking for us.
-> Why without Portugal?
    Antonio Manuel ne Vunda: Portugal liked to stand between Kongo and the world. Kongo wanted its own bishop and its own friends in Europe. So I went myself: by Brazil, by Spain, robbed by pirates on the way. It took four years.
-> What happened in Rome?
    Antonio Manuel ne Vunda: I arrived in January 1608, sick from the journey, and died a few days later. They buried me with honour and made a marble bust of me. It is still there. I did not see the city.
-> Goodbye.
    Antonio Manuel ne Vunda: If you reach Rome, tell them Kongo sent me.
===
title: history_nzinga
---
Nzinga: Stand up straight when you speak to a queen. Better.
-> Who are you?
    Nzinga: Nzinga Mbande, queen of Ndongo and Matamba. My father was king of Ndongo. My brother was king after him. When he needed someone to make peace with the Portuguese in 1622, he sent me.
-> Tell me about the chair.
    Nzinga: In Luanda the governor sat in a velvet chair and gave me a mat on the floor, as if I came to beg. I did not sit on his mat. One of my women knelt, and I sat on her back, and spoke to him eye to eye. I got my treaty.
-> And after?
    Nzinga: The Portuguese broke the treaty. I took Matamba, made war camps, allied with the Imbangala, and when the Dutch took Luanda in 1641 I allied with them too. I fought for thirty years. In 1657 Portugal made peace with me. I died a queen, at about eighty.
-> Goodbye.
    Nzinga: Go. And do not let anyone give you a mat.
===
title: history_funji
---
Kifunji: Speak quietly. The walls in Luanda have Portuguese ears. So do I.
-> Who are you?
    Kifunji: Kifunji, Nzinga's sister. The Portuguese captured me in 1646 and kept me in Luanda. They thought a prisoner was harmless.
-> What did you do?
    Kifunji: I wrote to my sister. I told her what the Portuguese planned, how many soldiers came, which ships sailed. My letters went out hidden, to her and to the Dutch.
-> What happened to you?
    Kifunji: They found the letters. The records say they drowned me in the Kwanza in 1647. My sister fought on. Remember that a prisoner can still fight.
-> Goodbye.
    Kifunji: Not a word to anyone.
===
title: history_george_washington_williams
---
George Washington Williams: You are an American? No? Then listen anyway. Someone must.
-> Who are you?
    George Washington Williams: A soldier of the Civil War at fourteen, then a minister, a lawyer, a member of the Ohio legislature, and the first historian of Black Americans. I came to the Congo in 1890 to see the paradise that King Leopold told the world about.
-> What did you find?
    George Washington Williams: Villages burned, people taken hostage, forced labour, killings; and none of the schools or hospitals the king had promised. I wrote him an open letter and listed it all, place by place.
-> What happened then?
    George Washington Williams: The king's friends called me a liar. To the American Secretary of State I wrote that these were 'crimes against humanity'. I died of tuberculosis in 1891, at forty-one. Others carried it on.
-> Goodbye.
    George Washington Williams: Write down what you see. Then publish it.
===
title: history_rubber_gatherer
---
Rubber Gatherer: I have no time. The basket must be full by the new moon.
-> What is the basket for?
    Rubber Gatherer: Rubber. The vine grows wild in the forest. We cut it, catch the sap, and let it dry on our skin and pull it off in strips. Each village owes so many baskets, every two weeks. The Company soldiers come to count.
-> And if the basket is not full?
    Rubber Gatherer: Then they take the women as hostages until it is. They burn the village. They shoot. The vines near us are dead now; we go further and further into the forest, for days.
-> Why rubber?
    Rubber Gatherer: For your bicycles, they say, and your new motor cars. Tyres. Somebody across the sea is riding very smoothly.
-> Goodbye.
    Rubber Gatherer: Tell them. That is all I ask.
===
title: history_william_sheppard
---
William Sheppard: Welcome. Mind the river; the steamer does not wait.
-> Who are you?
    William Sheppard: William Henry Sheppard, of Virginia: a Presbyterian missionary and explorer. I came here in 1890. I learned the Kuba language and was the first outsider received by their king. London's Royal Geographical Society made me a fellow.
-> What did you see?
    William Sheppard: In 1899 I was sent to find out why the villages were empty. I found a raiding camp of the State's soldiers and the dead of the villages they had raided. I counted them and wrote it down.
-> What then?
    William Sheppard: My report was printed in America and Britain. In 1909 the rubber company sued me for libel. A Congolese court found me not guilty. I took the Kuba's art home to Hampton, Virginia, and it is still there.
-> Goodbye.
    William Sheppard: God keep you. Keep your eyes open.
===
title: history_alice_seeley_harris
---
Alice Seeley Harris: Hold still. ... There. Thank you.
-> Who are you?
    Alice Seeley Harris: An English missionary. I came to the Congo with my husband John in 1898. I meant to teach. I ended up taking photographs.
-> Why photographs?
    Alice Seeley Harris: Because nobody in Europe believed the letters. In 1904 a man called Nsala came to our mission and sat in front of our house, with what the soldiers had done to his child. I photographed him. People can argue with a letter. It is harder to argue with a photograph.
-> What did you do with them?
    Alice Seeley Harris: We made them into slides for a magic lantern and showed them in hundreds of halls in Britain and America. With Edmund Morel and Roger Casement, the campaign forced the king to give up the Congo in 1908. I lived to be a hundred.
-> Goodbye.
    Alice Seeley Harris: Look. And then make others look.
===
title: history_mungo_fisher
---
Mungo Fisher: The lake is full this year. Sit, eat. There are more yabbies than we can carry.
-> Where are we?
    Mungo Fisher: On the shore of the lake, under the long white dune. Fish, mussels, yabbies, birds' eggs in season, emus that come to drink. We have lived here as long as our stories go, and they go a long way.
-> What are the shells?
    Mungo Fisher: Our meals. We eat the mussels by the fire and leave the shells in heaps. Your diggers read our heaps like a book: what we ate, and when.
-> Will the lake last?
    Mungo Fisher: The water comes and goes with the rains. One day it will go and not come back, and the dune will blow over our old camps. We will still be here. Ask the ranger: she is my great-granddaughter, two thousand grandmothers on.
-> Goodbye.
    Mungo Fisher: Take some fish for the road.
===
title: history_mungo_ranger
---
Mungo Ranger: Welcome to Mungo. Mind where you put your feet. Some of these footprints are twenty thousand years old.
-> Who are you?
    Mungo Ranger: A Mutthi Mutthi woman, and a ranger here. This is the country of three peoples: the Paakantyi, the Mutthi Mutthi and the Ngiyampaa. We look after it with the park, together.
-> What was found here?
    Mungo Ranger: In 1968 a geologist found burnt bones in the dune: a young woman, cremated about 40,000 years ago. In 1974, a man, laid in his grave and sprinkled with red ochre, about 42,000 years ago. Among the oldest ritual burials known anywhere in the world. We call them Mungo Lady and Mungo Man.
-> Where are they now?
    Mungo Ranger: Mungo Lady came back to us in 1992. Mungo Man was kept in Canberra for 43 years; in 2017 we brought him home in a hearse across the country, and people stood by the road. Our old people are home. That matters more than any record.
-> Goodbye.
    Mungo Ranger: Walk softly. You are a guest here.
===
title: history_tuitatui
---
Tuʻitātui: Stand back, the stone is heavy. Each upright weighs more than thirty tonnes.
-> Who are you?
    Tuʻitātui: Tuʻitātui, the eleventh Tuʻi Tonga. My line goes back to the god of the sky, so they say. My canoes take my word to Samoa, to Fiji, to the islands beyond.
-> What is the stone gate?
    Tuʻitātui: Haʻamonga ʻa Maui: the burden of Maui, two uprights and a lintel of coral stone. Some say it was a gate to my court; some say the marks on top point to where the sun rises at the turning of the year. A king of Tonga thought so, in 1967, and was out at dawn to watch.
-> Why is Tonga different?
    Tuʻitātui: Ask the Europeans. They came, they traded, they argued. But no one ever took Tonga. Britain 'protected' us for seventy years, but no one ever ruled Tonga. We are still a kingdom.
-> Goodbye.
    Tuʻitātui: Go well, and bow to the stone.
===
title: history_ngatu_maker
---
Ngatu Maker: Thud, thud, thud. You hear that across the village in the morning? That is us.
-> What are you making?
    Ngatu Maker: Ngatu: bark cloth. We strip the inner bark of the paper mulberry, soak it, and beat it thin and wide with the ike, the wooden beater. Then we glue the pieces together and print the patterns in brown.
-> How big?
    Ngatu Maker: For a wedding or a funeral, as long as you like. Some are tens of metres, carried by a line of women. A great ngatu is wealth: you give it away, and everyone remembers.
-> Who makes it?
    Ngatu Maker: The women, together, singing and talking. A ngatu holds the gossip of the whole village. Do not ask what it says.
-> Goodbye.
    Ngatu Maker: Come back when it is printed.
===
title: history_tufuga_tatau
---
Tufuga Tā Tatau: You want a tatau? Sit. It will hurt. It is supposed to.
-> What do you do?
    Tufuga Tā Tatau: I am a master of tatau. I tap the ink in with a comb of bone and a little mallet: tap, tap, tap. A man's peʻa goes from the waist to the knees. It takes days, and he must not cry out.
-> Where does the word come from?
    Tufuga Tā Tatau: From our language: tatau. The English sailors heard it in Tahiti and took it home, and so did the ink. Now half the world has a tattoo and does not know whose word it is.
-> Why does it matter?
    Tufuga Tā Tatau: A peʻa says that you are a grown man and that you serve your family. The missionaries tried to stop it. They could not. Our tatau never stopped, in all the centuries.
-> Goodbye.
    Tufuga Tā Tatau: Come back when you are brave.
===
title: history_moa_hunter
---
Moa Hunter: Shh. There is a moa in the bush, a big one, taller than two of you.
-> What is a moa?
    Moa Hunter: A bird with no wings at all, nine kinds of them, the tallest taller than any man. Before we came there was nothing here to hunt them but the great eagle. They did not know to run from us.
-> Is the hunting good?
    Moa Hunter: Too good. Every family eats moa, and their eggs. My grandfather's fires were full of moa bones. Mine have fewer. My grandchildren will have to ask what one was.
-> What happens to them?
    Moa Hunter: They are gone within about a hundred and fifty years of our coming. Then the eagle, which had nothing left to hunt. It was not done on purpose. That is what makes it a warning.
-> Goodbye.
    Moa Hunter: Walk quietly. While there are still moa to hear you.
===
title: history_pounamu_carver
---
Pounamu Carver: Grind, wet, grind, wet. One more week and this adze will be sharp.
-> What is that stone?
    Pounamu Carver: Pounamu, greenstone, from the rivers of the South Island. It is tougher than any other stone we know. It makes the best adzes, chisels and ornaments, and the finest weapons.
-> Why is it so precious?
    Pounamu Carver: Because it is hard to find and very hard to work: weeks of grinding with sand and water for one blade. A pounamu adze is handed down for generations. It has a name, and a story.
-> Where are we?
    Pounamu Carver: In Aotearoa, the land of the long white cloud. Our ancestors came by waka from the islands in the north and east, about a hundred years ago, following the stars and the birds. Ask the elders about Kupe.
-> Goodbye.
    Pounamu Carver: Haere rā. Go well.
===
title: history_henry_williams
---
Henry Williams: Forgive me, I have not slept. We were translating all night.
-> Translating what?
    Henry Williams: The Treaty. Governor Hobson wrote it in English on the 4th, and my son Edward and I put it into Māori by morning. The chiefs are to sign on the 6th. I have lived here seventeen years. I know the language. I hope I chose the words well.
-> What words?
    Henry Williams: 'Sovereignty' has no word in Māori. I wrote 'kāwanatanga', governorship, which the chiefs know from the Bible: a governor, under a king. And I wrote that they keep 'tino rangatiratanga', their full chieftainship, over their lands and treasures.
-> Was that the same thing?
    Henry Williams: Many say no, and they are right that it is not. The English text has the Queen take sovereignty. The Māori text, which almost all the chiefs signed, does not. New Zealand has argued about it ever since, and in 1975 set up a tribunal to settle it.
-> Goodbye.
    Henry Williams: Good day. I am going to sleep.
===
title: history_ana_hamu
---
Ana Hamu: You are looking at the paper. So was I.
-> Who are you?
    Ana Hamu: Ana Hamu. My husband, Te Koki, was a chief of this bay. He is dead, so I speak for our people and our land, and I signed the Treaty for them.
-> Did many women sign?
    Ana Hamu: A few, here and in other places: a dozen or so, out of more than five hundred names. Some women of rank were turned away by British officers. A chief is a chief, I say.
-> Why did you sign?
    Ana Hamu: The paper in our language says we keep our chieftainship and our lands, and the Queen keeps order among the white people. That seemed fair. Whether it was kept is another story.
-> Goodbye.
    Ana Hamu: Go well, and read the Māori text.
===
title: history_hone_heke
---
Hōne Heke: You want to see my axe? Ah, you want to see the flagstaff. Same thing, really.
-> Who are you?
    Hōne Heke: I am Hōne Heke Pōkai, a chief of Ngāpuhi. At Waitangi I was the first of the chiefs to sign the Treaty. I spoke for it. I thought it was a covenant between us and the Queen.
-> And the flagstaff?
    Hōne Heke: The British flag on the hill at Kororāreka. Within a few years the trade had left our bay, there were new customs and duties, and the flag stood there saying who was master. So in July 1844 I cut it down. They put it up again. I cut it down again. Four times.
-> Four times?
    Hōne Heke: The fourth time they sheathed it in iron and put soldiers round it. We cut it down anyway, in March 1845, and the war of the north began. The British never raised that flag there again in my lifetime.
-> Goodbye.
    Hōne Heke: Mind the flagstaff. It falls over a lot.
===
title: history_barangaroo
---
Barangaroo: You are with the ones in the big canoes? Then stay out of my way. I am fishing.
-> Who are you?
    Barangaroo: Barangaroo, of the Cammeraygal, on the north shore of the harbour. I fish from my nawi with my daughters, with a little fire in the canoe to cook what we catch. My line is bark fibre; my hook is shell, ground and shaped.
-> What do you think of the newcomers?
    Barangaroo: They take more fish in one net than the harbour can give. They put up fences. They call the land empty, and we are standing on it. My husband Bennelong goes to dine with their governor. I do not. I will not wear their clothes, and I will not eat at their table.
-> What happens to you?
    Barangaroo: I die in 1791, after my child is born. Bennelong burns my body in our way, and buries the ashes in the governor's garden. Part of the harbour shore carries my name now. Good. Let them say it every day.
-> Goodbye.
    Barangaroo: Go. The fish are biting.
===
title: history_bennelong
---
Bennelong: Good day to you, sir or madam. You see, I have learned their manners. They have not learned mine.
-> Who are you?
    Bennelong: Woollarawarre Bennelong, of the Wangal. In November 1789 the governor's men seized me and kept me in a chain, so I would teach them our language and our ways. I escaped. Later I came back, on my terms.
-> What did you do?
    Bennelong: I went between the two peoples. The governor built me a hut on the point where their Opera House stands now. In 1792 I sailed with him to England, saw their London and their winter, and came home in 1795, sick and tired of their country.
-> What happened to your people?
    Bennelong: In 1789 smallpox came through the harbour, and perhaps half of the people of the harbour died. The rest saw their fishing grounds taken. I lived to 1813. Some of them laughed at me in my coat. They were not wrong. Neither was I.
-> Goodbye.
    Bennelong: Good day. Or as we say: go well on your country, if they let you.
===
title: history_pemulwuy
---
Pemulwuy: Stay where you are. I see you.
-> Who are you?
    Pemulwuy: Pemulwuy, of the Bidjigal, on the Georges River. The newcomers take our land for their farms, and their guns take our people. So we fight them, as anyone would.
-> How long?
    Pemulwuy: Twelve years, from 1790. We burn their crops, take their corn and fight their soldiers. They shot me many times. They said I could not be killed. In 1802 they killed me.
-> And after?
    Pemulwuy: The governor sent my head to England, to a man who collected such things. It has never been found. My son Tedbury fought on. The wars on the frontier went on, place by place, across the whole country, for more than a hundred years.
-> Goodbye.
    Pemulwuy: Go. Remember whose land you walk on.
===
title: history_kooyang_farmer
---
Kooyang Farmer: Quiet. The water is rising, and the kooyang are moving.
-> What are you doing?
    Kooyang Farmer: Setting the trap in the weir. When the rains come, the eels run down the channels to the sea. The weir sends them into the gap, and the gap into my basket.
-> Who made the channels?
    Kooyang Farmer: Our old people, cutting and moving the stone of the lava, for longer than anyone can count. Your diggers say at least 6,600 years. Some channels hold eels for months, in ponds, so we can take them when we need them. That is farming. Do not let anyone tell you otherwise.
-> And the volcano?
    Kooyang Farmer: Budj Bim. Our stories say an ancestor being rose up there, and the land was made. Your scientists say it erupted about 37,000 years ago. People were here to see it. We are still here.
-> Goodbye.
    Kooyang Farmer: Go well. Step on the stones, not in the channels.
===
title: history_basket_weaver
---
Basket Weaver: Over, under, over, under. Hold this end for me.
-> What are you making?
    Basket Weaver: An eel trap: long and narrow, with a neck the eel can swim into and not out of. Sedge from the swamp, twisted and plaited. A good one lasts many seasons.
-> What happens to the eels?
    Basket Weaver: We eat them fresh, or smoke them in the hollow trees, and they keep. People come from far away to trade for smoked kooyang. Our country fed many.
-> What happened later?
    Basket Weaver: The squatters came in the 1830s with sheep and guns, and the Gunditjmara fought them for years: the Eumeralla wars. Later the lake was drained for farms. In 2010 our people brought the water back, and in 2019 the world listed this place among its heritage. We manage it now.
-> Goodbye.
    Basket Weaver: Come back in the eel season. Bring an appetite.
===
title: history_latte_builder
---
Latte Builder: Mind your head. This house stands on stone legs, and so will my grandchildren's.
-> What are the stones?
    Latte Builder: Latte: a pillar, the haligi, and a cap on top, the tasa, shaped like half a coconut. We set them in two rows and build the house on top. Rats cannot climb the caps, and the sea wind blows underneath.
-> How did you get here?
    Latte Builder: Our ancestors sailed from the islands to the west, more than three thousand years ago, across open sea further than anyone had gone before. Then they kept sailing. We still do. Look at the sakman: the fastest canoe your sailors will ever see.
-> Goodbye.
    Latte Builder: Håfa adai. Come up to the house.
===
title: history_hurao
---
Hurao: You came with the priests and the soldiers? No? Good. Sit.
-> Who are you?
    Hurao: Hurao, a chief of Guåhan. In 1671 the Spanish had been on our island three years. They brought sickness, and their soldiers did as they liked. I told our people what I believed: that we had lived happily before they came, and we could live without them.
-> What happened?
    Hurao: We fought. They had guns and ships; we had slings and our own land. The wars went on for more than twenty years. Then the sickness came with them, and most of us died. The Spanish moved the survivors into towns, away from the latte.
-> And now?
    Hurao: The CHamoru are still here. Our language is still spoken. Guåhan has had three flags over it since. Ask whose island it is.
-> Goodbye.
    Hurao: Go. And remember who was here first.
===
title: history_basalt_hauler
---
Basalt Hauler: Hup. Hup. Put your shoulder under the other end, will you?
-> What is that?
    Basalt Hauler: A column of basalt, from quarries elsewhere on Pohnpei; nobody now knows exactly where. The rock cools into six-sided columns, as if someone cut them. We lay them crosswise, one course one way and the next the other, and fill the middle with coral.
-> How did you get them here?
    Basalt Hauler: Rafts, ropes, and many people. And the Saudeleur, who ruled us and liked to be obeyed. The stories say two brothers flew the stones here by magic. I wish they would fly this one.
-> Goodbye.
    Basalt Hauler: Mind the canals. The tide is coming in.
===
title: history_isokelekel
---
Isokelekel: Stand easy. The Saudeleur are finished.
-> Who are you?
    Isokelekel: Isokelekel. The stories say I came from Kosrae with my men, because the Saudeleur had grown cruel and greedy. They took every first catch and every first fruit for themselves. After a hard fight, their rule ended, here in Nan Madol.
-> What did you do then?
    Isokelekel: I became the first Nahnmwarki. The island was divided among chiefs, with titles that pass down. They still do: Pohnpei has its Nahnmwarki today, and the feasts with sakau go on.
-> And the city?
    Isokelekel: In time people left it. The jungle and the mangroves grew over the walls. It is still the place of the old ones. Walk carefully, and be respectful.
-> Goodbye.
    Isokelekel: Kaselehlie. Go well.
===
title: history_ri_meto
---
Ri-meto: Close your eyes. Feel the canoe. Do you feel the swell under you?
-> What is this chart?
    Ri-meto: Sticks for the swells and shells for the islands. A swell from the east hits an island and bends around it, and on the other side it meets itself. Where the swells cross, the canoe rocks in a special way. That tells you an island is near, even when you cannot see it.
-> Do you take the chart to sea?
    Ri-meto: Never. You learn it on land, from your teacher, and then you leave it at home. At sea you read the water with your body. I lie down in the canoe to feel it better.
-> Who learns?
    Ri-meto: Only a few, from one family to the next. It was nearly lost. Today people are learning it again, from the old navigators who still knew.
-> Goodbye.
    Ri-meto: Go well. The swell from the east is strong today.
===
title: history_pandanus_weaver
---
Pandanus Weaver: Strip, soften, plait. A mat for sleeping, a mat for a sail, a mat for a wedding.
-> A sail of mats?
    Pandanus Weaver: Of course. Pandanus leaves, plaited fine and sewn together. The canoes of the Marshalls carry the mats of the women. A navigator is nothing without a good sail.
-> What is life on an atoll like?
    Pandanus Weaver: A ring of sand and palms around a lagoon, two metres above the sea. Coconuts, breadfruit, pandanus, fish, and each other. We know every family on every atoll, and whose land is whose. Land is everything here, because there is so little of it.
-> Goodbye.
    Pandanus Weaver: Iakwe. Come back for the wedding.
===
title: history_rai_owner
---
Rai Owner: You are standing in front of my money. Do not lean on it.
-> That stone is money?
    Rai Owner: Rai. That one is mine, and it has stood there since my grandfather's time. When I buy something big, the stone does not move. Everyone just knows that now it belongs to someone else. The whole island keeps the account.
-> How much is it worth?
    Rai Owner: It depends on its story. Who carved it in Palau, how many men died bringing it home, how bad the storm was. A small stone with a terrible voyage is worth more than a big one that came easily.
-> And the one at the bottom of the sea?
    Rai Owner: A family's stone sank on the way home, long ago. Everyone agreed it was still theirs, so it still is. Nobody alive has seen it. It is still spent sometimes.
-> Goodbye.
    Rai Owner: Go well. Do not tell the Germans. Last year they fined my village by painting marks on our stones.
===
title: history_palau_quarrier
---
Palau Quarrier: Cough. Sorry. Limestone dust. I have been in Palau for half a year, carving.
-> Why Palau?
    Palau Quarrier: Yap has no stone like this. Palau does, and its chiefs let us quarry it if we work for them too. We carve with shell adzes, months for one big stone. Then we take it home: 400 km of open sea, on a raft behind a canoe.
-> And the iron tools?
    Palau Quarrier: An Irish-American, O'Keefe, came in 1871 with a ship and iron tools, and brought stones by the dozen for copra. People took them. But a stone with no hardship in it is worth less. Everyone could see it.
-> Goodbye.
    Palau Quarrier: Go well. If you see a stone floating, it is not mine.
===
title: history_phosphate_digger
---
Phosphate Digger: Dig between the pinnacles, fill the basket, carry it to the line. Again.
-> Who are you?
    Phosphate Digger: A labourer from Guangdong, on a contract for three years. The British Phosphate Commissioners bring us from China and from the islands. We live in the camps; the pay goes home.
-> What is phosphate?
    Phosphate Digger: Old bird droppings, turned to rock over a long, long time. Farmers in Australia and New Zealand spread it on their fields. Their sheep grow fat on this island.
-> What is left?
    Phosphate Digger: These pinnacles. Coral, with nothing between it. Nothing grows up here now. The Nauruans watched their island go away by ship, a little more every day.
-> Goodbye.
    Phosphate Digger: Mind your step. There is a long way down between the stones.
===
title: history_hammer_deroburt
---
Hammer DeRoburt: Good day. Excuse the files: I am taking a government to court.
-> Who are you?
    Hammer DeRoburt: Hammer DeRoburt, head chief of Nauru. In 1943 the Japanese sent me with 1,200 of our people to Chuuk. Hundreds died there. When the survivors came home in 1946, I decided that Nauruans would decide what happened to Nauru.
-> What did you do?
    Hammer DeRoburt: I bargained with Australia, Britain and New Zealand for twenty years. On 31 January 1968 Nauru became independent, the smallest republic in the world. In 1970 we bought the phosphate company. For a while we were among the richest people on Earth, per head.
-> And the land?
    Hammer DeRoburt: The land they took before 1968 was gone. So in 1989 we took Australia to the International Court of Justice. They settled in 1993. Money does not grow coral back.
-> Goodbye.
    Hammer DeRoburt: Good day. I have a court date.
===
title: history_juda
---
Juda: Please, sit. We are packing. There is not much time.
-> Who are you?
    Juda: Juda Kessibuki, the leader of the people of Bikini. A commodore of the American navy came after church and asked us to leave our island for a while, so they could test a bomb that would end all wars. He said it was for the good of mankind.
-> What did you say?
    Juda: We talked for a long time. Then I said that we would go. What else could we say to a nation that had just won a war? We are 167 people. They had ships, and nearly a hundred warships coming to anchor in our lagoon, as targets.
-> Will you come back?
    Juda: They told us it would be for a short time. Ask your history books how long it has been.
-> Goodbye.
    Juda: Go well. Look after the island, if you can.
===
title: history_bikini_elder
---
Bikini Elder: My mother's mat, my grandmother's mat. Everything rolls up small, if you have to leave.
-> Where are you going?
    Bikini Elder: To Rongerik, they say. It is smaller than Bikini, and its lagoon has few fish. They say we will be fine. They have not been there.
-> What happened there?
    Bikini Elder: We nearly starved. In 1948 they moved us again, to Kili, an island with no lagoon at all, where we could not fish in our way. Our children grew up far from Bikini. In 1968 they said Bikini was safe; some went back. In 1978 they found it was not, and moved us again.
-> Goodbye.
    Bikini Elder: Remember our island's name for what it was. Not for a swimsuit.
===
